<!-- cell: note id="9797dc34-5962-43ff-aab0-4a23cfea7922" -->

## The politician's deal

How a corrupt politician used Captain Varn to win Mars for his backers, fell for her, and lost everything.

1. The politician sets out to win a mafia-tied mining company exclusive rights to Mars, and befriends Captain Varn, who commands the mission's escort, for what she can tell him and the weight she gives him.
2. He passes on what Varn tells him, and uses it to push the committee into faster and deeper digging.
3. When the alien fleet arrives, the company makes him keep the digging going, and his love for Varn turns against his deal.
4. The excavation is lost to the war, and the evidence from the custody case exposes him: he loses the deal, his seat and Varn.

<!-- cell: plot id="3e1100db-02f4-434a-af42-9e9c3cf08843" title="The politician's deal" edges="90af46c5-8935-484b-9e2d-abb68c62c8ab:589f3663-64dc-4aec-ad1e-493a8927f191>8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4, 7b5149a2-d430-406d-87a6-a47ca5ccd7dc:8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4>ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2, bab104ec-a35e-47a0-94e4-fa1b450bf3c6:ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2>6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617, 012949a1-cc62-48b6-befe-e4e1c4aa2c5f:6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617>faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507, 477ea5f0-3a6b-46da-a336-c15a53fe2e82:faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507>67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce, 8f48dd3e-34f8-4e51-a872-627347c11a3e:67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce>198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b, fd70ff4f-22b1-4d6f-90e6-104971076b73:198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b>9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8, fc67428f-78ef-437b-acb5-3b162b0ed2b2:9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8>2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0, 2c20b181-7179-4df5-b3b5-0bd1ebdf21ea:2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0>6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf, 08354c6b-b2c5-48b8-a882-c709e76511c4:6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf>fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5, c94d3dda-36ed-4d1e-bc58-423e87893859:fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5>20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04, 5b9efebb-935a-4d69-9f0d-1779894a06b6:20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04>a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c, 2be0883d-fcc7-4be9-8a73-526869905e9b:a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c>4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722, afe0500e-e187-43ea-a53a-edbc76998d46:4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722>1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123, a7a17ba3-6402-4138-9f24-61c23e789fc0:1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123>5d242200-e8fe-439b-8513-b62f37a3ecda" -->

<ol title="A politician in the pay of a mafia-tied mining company befriends Captain Varn to learn what the government knows about Mars, keeps the digging going for his backers even when the alien fleet arrives, and is exposed and ruined as the war begins.">
<li id="589f3663-64dc-4aec-ad1e-493a8927f191" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="1648-2078">The politician, who sits on the committee that oversees the space agency, uses his meetings with Captain Varn to learn what the government is doing on Mars, and is not telling the committee.</li>
<li id="8422be60-7d37-42d3-a1f1-6f0f82555ba4" timestamp="1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="3476-3904">The owners of a mining company with ties to the mafia, who paid for the politician's career, tell him to win them exclusive rights to mine Mars, now that something has been found there.</li>
<li id="ca180136-4258-4d25-a292-3af0d6137af2" timestamp="1.3" location="earth, parliament" words="5697-6086">He puts the company's bid for an exclusive licence to the committee, but the other members will not commit until they know what was found.</li>
<li id="6e2e062f-d5fe-46ef-a477-b3a02db36617" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="6630-7037">He tells the owners that Varn will be his eyes on Mars, and that through her he will know more than anyone on the committee.</li>
<li id="faf4f244-09a4-4ba8-8562-ba1fff92e507" timestamp="2.1" location="orbit" words="7597-8028">On their calls, Varn tells him that the object is unlike any rock, and that the agency is keeping what it finds quiet. He passes it on to the company.</li>
<li id="67d53dfb-4625-4a74-9585-00b066fe60ce" timestamp="2.2.1" location="earth, parliament" words="10479-10889">With what Varn told him, he persuades the committee to order faster and deeper digging, with machines the company supplies.</li>
<li id="198e68d9-b03d-4513-bb1e-240d50fe333b" timestamp="2.3.1" location="earth, parliament" words="12378-12771">Members who cannot get the agency's findings come to him for them, and the company's licence gains a majority.</li>
<li id="9e4e9f1f-7654-40d9-a654-458acdb305c8" timestamp="3.1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="14262-14645">The owners order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, so that whatever is in the ground is theirs before anyone can stop it.</li>
<li id="2fa3fe8a-cab8-4827-bb25-43ab957c1af0" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, parliament" words="15174-15641">Against the members who want the excavation halted, he talks the committee into ordering the mission to protect the discovery and dig on, which keeps Varn's corvette in the fleet's path.</li>
<li id="6ea45030-a6f2-4529-9465-0ae2a297ddaf" timestamp="3.3" location="orbit" words="16623-17165">Varn tells him that her corvette cannot stop a fleet. He understands that the order he fought for may cost her her life.</li>
<li id="fad9aa0b-ff15-4826-9e82-1bfde9b2dcc5" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="18181-18603">His concerns show the owners that he has grown attached to Varn, and they begin to doubt him.</li>
<li id="20042bf3-b404-4347-a71d-3753222c0e04" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, parliament" words="20035-20398">Word reaches the committee that his name, and his ties to the company, have come up in a court hearing over the custody of Varn's daughter.</li>
<li id="a5a49d44-dad4-4333-8212-212220328d0c" timestamp="4.1.1" location="earth, parliament" words="22775-23102">The committee suspends him while his ties to the company are investigated.</li>
<li id="4411bd09-df3e-4c5a-a5e3-3dd672641722" timestamp="4.3" location="earth, the company's offices" words="24506-24889">The owners fire him: he has grown too attached to Varn, and become a liability.</li>
<li id="1f1c7e43-2b23-4f69-a91b-80a28775b123" timestamp="4.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="25387-25799">The company loses contact with its excavators on Mars, and with them the licence it was promised.</li>
<li id="5d242200-e8fe-439b-8513-b62f37a3ecda" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="26674-27129">He learns that Earth's ships at Mars, Varn's corvette among them, are gone, and that Earth calls it an act of war.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="397a460f-3636-47fb-9687-f747fed675bc" -->

## The politician and Captain Varn

How a politician pretended to like Captain Varn, and came to love her.

1. The politician meets Captain Varn and pretends to like her, to get into her good graces. She tells him about her daughter, and that she is not looking for romance; he keeps playing coy.
2. When Varn is sent to Mars, he arranges remote dates. They start talking about the future, and she asks him to look in on her daughter.
3. A day with her daughter reminds him of his own childhood, and he is strangely moved. When his overseers want Varn pushed closer to the object, he voices concerns.
4. As the alien fleet closes in, in a last desperate call, he realizes he loves her, and promises to look after her daughter.

<!-- cell: plot id="54d2a399-e38c-4f8e-8ba7-22e965ec879a" title="The politician and Captain Varn" edges="1d6ffa76-53b4-4551-bbbd-d4eef96091fe:b27fcca3-cf80-4241-b658-9a505feb075e>48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858, ea4fcbbf-0257-43f0-9c3f-2d2767eebfd7:48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858>374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9, 3edfc3a6-7c5e-4032-99da-b82b12abfed6:374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9>d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7, f25de376-eaec-469f-a60b-61e859174605:d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7>2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f, 75031a43-cfbb-4833-ab12-1301068ca59c:2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f>604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e, 9cc74a17-ad4d-4ab9-859e-485620632ebc:604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e>1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd, 495e4e72-9a07-4e10-8a94-24aef9132427:1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd>b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038, 25d9cba0-e7c2-4d5e-80c0-bbc58719ee79:b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038>5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3, c2bbc7c4-a301-4caa-bcd8-0f0785a8abd3:5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3>c3e8b8f1-dad3-460d-b9e8-a529481ff041" -->

<ol title="A politician courts Captain Varn for what she can tell him, keeps up remote dates when she is sent to Mars, is moved by a day with her daughter, and, as the alien fleet closes in, realizes he loves her and promises to look after the girl.">
<li id="b27fcca3-cf80-4241-b658-9a505feb075e" timestamp="0.1" location="earth, command center" words="775-1175">The politician meets Captain Varn, and starts pretending to like her, to get into her good graces.</li>
<li id="48e7772f-91a8-4a29-aeed-e43711d3e858" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="1648-2078">He invites her to dinner. She tells him about her daughter, and that she is not looking for romance.</li>
<li id="374c0da6-5fff-4063-bbf5-630fdbbacdb9" timestamp="0.3" location="earth, a restaurant" words="2197-2607">They keep meeting, and he keeps playing coy.</li>
<li id="d95e8bae-814f-440c-8313-2c1fe2eec0d7" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="6630-7037">He learns that Varn is being sent with the mission to Mars: a relief, and at first an opportunity to learn more about the mysterious object.</li>
<li id="2a276b38-e6d9-4db1-aef5-15c942b2ae6f" timestamp="2.1" location="orbit" words="7597-8028">With Varn in space, he arranges remote dates: he calls her, and they start talking about the future.</li>
<li id="604ce2a6-1ab6-4bdc-ba72-7b7526d6562e" timestamp="2.2" location="orbit" words="9092-9497">On one of their calls, Varn asks him to check on her daughter, and he agrees.</li>
<li id="1d2cbbcf-2c53-48ff-a775-9fa5493261dd" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, Varn's house" words="11357-11820">He spends a day with her daughter, taking care of her. Among the photos of Varn and the stories the girl tells about her, something reminds him of his own childhood.</li>
<li id="b84d945c-ea9e-4266-874c-62fea7bec038" timestamp="3.1" location="orbit" words="12823-13171">The next time he calls Varn, he is strangely moved.</li>
<li id="5413d17f-ab61-45b0-8bf5-1a628fd98fc3" timestamp="3.1.1" location="earth, the company's offices" words="14262-14645">When his overseers order him to push Varn to investigate the object more closely, he voices concerns.</li>
<li id="c3e8b8f1-dad3-460d-b9e8-a529481ff041" timestamp="4.1" location="orbit" words="21823-22342">As the alien fleet closes in, in a last desperate call with Varn, he realizes that he loves her, and promises to look after her daughter.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="46a0a8ec-de09-415a-bf2b-498fb9518907" -->

## The detective hired against Captain Varn

How a detective hired to find dirt on Captain Varn came to take her side, and helped her daughter to the man she trusted.

1. Captain Varn's ex-husband hires a detective to find dirt on her. He finds nothing on her, but sees a politician courting her, and follows him to a company with ties to the mafia.
2. His client's glee at anything that will hurt Varn troubles him, and he starts looking into the client instead. Watching the politician look after Varn's daughter, he sees a bond forming, and asks Varn to meet him.
3. He finds that Varn is the better parent, and that the father only wants to hurt her. As his own photographs are used against her in court, he cancels the contract and sides with Varn.
4. When Varn's ship is destroyed, he helps the politician win custody of her daughter.

<!-- cell: plot id="d747aff2-bac6-4448-a95e-2b00d83c5f0d" title="The detective hired against Captain Varn" edges="831ce51a-19e7-4614-8a50-c4de9de9fb67:ecfd224a-7ab6-4397-8f49-6fe043e233c1>213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac, cb54ba22-e80c-4aef-a96c-ff1294cb90ba:213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac>508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5, b111f900-09ca-4f24-b8ed-6d338ee254bd:508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5>41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e, 103a36ba-110e-4a00-b6c7-88104477181c:41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e>b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3, 155f4eae-86a0-433b-bff2-f7b0bc21869d:b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3>85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb, b883d1f3-9a7a-493f-b640-8912f8c26cd0:85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb>7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89, fceb5632-5ffe-4499-929b-638eba75e5f8:7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89>f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f, 7e0626ca-cb2b-4048-9e01-85d07c58095a:f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f>b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad, 4ffc1f56-a07a-4682-8f07-d0ce7e721c86:b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad>7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402, c685f3e7-908e-4928-a445-631665745967:7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402>d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80, 74a974b1-cdd7-4ec9-aa18-9331e1da67bb:d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80>edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff, e55329cf-10bf-4251-bdc3-fa0f41d7ec64:edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff>1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b, 389e2686-960d-4936-b850-3f643b4fb05b:1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b>b234cb66-d2dc-4a51-978d-fcbbfdb3b796" -->

<ol title="A detective hired by Captain Varn's ex-husband finds nothing on her, is troubled by his client's wish to hurt her, finds that she is the better parent and the father only wants revenge, cancels his contract, and, when her ship is destroyed, helps the politician win custody of her daughter.">
<li id="ecfd224a-7ab6-4397-8f49-6fe043e233c1" timestamp="0.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="1220-1590">Captain Varn's ex-husband, who is not over her and wants custody of their daughter, hires the detective to find dirt on her.</li>
<li id="213dd038-fb32-48ad-90b4-63f3f78e35ac" timestamp="0.2" location="earth, a restaurant" words="1648-2078">Following Varn, the detective watches her dine with a man he recognizes: a politician on the committee that oversees the space agency.</li>
<li id="508750bb-0570-4732-a097-ec2d251e25f5" timestamp="0.3" location="earth, a restaurant" words="2197-2607">He photographs their meetings, but finds nothing on Varn: she drinks little, talks about her daughter, and goes home alone.</li>
<li id="41317c82-ab7b-4df6-8c17-e33939448a3e" timestamp="1.4" location="earth, the company's offices" words="6630-7037">Told by his client to dig into the man, he tails the politician to the offices of a mining company known for its ties to the mafia.</li>
<li id="b615a6e6-19d5-453f-a3d7-f2e42276d7c3" timestamp="2.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="8146-8555">He hands his client the photographs and the company's name. The man is glad of anything that will hurt Varn, and never once asks about his daughter. For the first time, the detective is uneasy.</li>
<li id="85c4d4ab-e749-42dd-9451-94eb366ce7bb" timestamp="2.2" location="earth, the detective's office" words="9568-9957">Instead of looking for more on Varn, he starts looking into his client.</li>
<li id="7ff121b2-0d29-4e9b-b12c-f6e8fc012f89" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, Varn's house" words="11357-11820">Watching Varn's house, he sees the politician spend the day looking after her daughter, and a bond forming between them.</li>
<li id="f6d510af-2eb5-46e9-a1aa-2385f1c38b7f" timestamp="3.1" location="earth, the detective's office" words="13273-13663">He sends Varn a message on her ship, asking to meet her when she can.</li>
<li id="b1c6a60a-d757-4ad9-a788-c60803b617ad" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, the daughter's school" words="15692-16125">From the girl's teachers and Varn's neighbours, he learns that Varn has raised her daughter alone, and raised her well.</li>
<li id="7e266d4a-1d4d-450e-bcaa-be35b6f5d402" timestamp="3.3" location="earth, the detective's office" words="17275-17639">He finds that the father has hardly seen his daughter in years, and has told his friends that the custody case is about making Varn pay.</li>
<li id="d8092fc5-4dea-43a6-a8e1-c68dde5a9d80" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the court" words="18656-19107">He sits in on the custody hearing, where the photographs he took are used to paint Varn as the companion of a man tied to the mafia.</li>
<li id="edb80954-ecf5-4ec2-8d2a-37bdc28790ff" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, the detective's office" words="20453-20907">He cancels his contract with Varn's ex-husband, and sides with Varn.</li>
<li id="1705e003-c8a9-41a9-9ff6-a25d3f36902b" timestamp="4.2" location="earth, the politician's office" words="23595-24027">He brings the politician what he has found: that Varn is the better parent, and that the father only wants to hurt her.</li>
<li id="b234cb66-d2dc-4a51-978d-fcbbfdb3b796" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="26674-27129">When Varn's ship is destroyed, he puts his evidence before the court for the politician, so that her daughter goes to the man Varn trusted with her, not to her father.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: note id="68dd0411-78f1-4cd8-9f16-b123a091cfdc" -->

## The custody of Captain Varn's daughter

How a lawyer fought to take Captain Varn's daughter from her, and lost the girl to the man Varn trusted.

1. Armed with the detective's evidence, Captain Varn's ex-husband hires a lawyer and sues for custody of their daughter while Varn is away on her mission to Mars.
2. At the hearings, Varn has to answer from her ship over a delayed link. The lawyer argues her dangerous assignment and her ties to a politician linked to the mafia, and the court leans toward the father.
3. Varn's ship is lost before the ruling. The politician asks for custody as Varn wished, the detective's evidence shows that the father only wanted to hurt her, and the court gives the girl to the politician.

<!-- cell: plot id="8f4f71f1-37ca-4d57-a0cf-45471d855f9a" title="The custody of Captain Varn's daughter" edges="baea2afe-a380-488b-8a6c-fe480a595662:3de9da00-a114-44c2-b928-5a9a4607b20e>125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5, 6707810e-3776-4402-b17f-f2d07b04a8c3:125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5>cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636, 750b8823-44dd-4838-901d-a782ca0568bc:cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636>74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30, dfbe00c4-7b1b-4dcb-b8a0-d787076ab381:74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30>c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9, fac08b49-6ac7-4efa-aa6e-7fea5e450c82:c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9>e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b, 546955cd-360f-42f8-b501-205d64cb7154:e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b>dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f, 1259a8c7-0369-4a37-9163-23d3ddcda356:dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f>4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74, 93f1d7a7-bd76-466a-9d1b-c096ed1f94e3:4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74>145b23ba-cb74-4c19-bd9e-d9b934bc245f" -->

<ol title="Hired by Captain Varn's ex-husband, a lawyer sues for custody of their daughter while Varn is at Mars, turns the court against her with the detective's photographs, and, when Varn's ship is lost, sees the girl given to the politician once the father is shown to have only wanted revenge.">
<li id="3de9da00-a114-44c2-b928-5a9a4607b20e" timestamp="2.2" location="earth, the lawyer's office" words="9997-10435">Captain Varn's ex-husband brings a lawyer the detective's photographs, and hires her to win him custody of his daughter.</li>
<li id="125e3aff-9837-4abf-b289-527ba3e949f5" timestamp="2.3" location="earth, the court" words="11933-12325">The lawyer sues for custody: the mother is away on a dangerous mission, and keeps company with a man tied to the mafia.</li>
<li id="cd62e9ff-c532-4813-89af-554fcbcab636" timestamp="3.1" location="earth, the court" words="13719-14218">At the first hearing, Varn has to answer from her ship over a delayed link. Her answers arrive minutes late, and the court hears them as evasions.</li>
<li id="74048a74-1b5e-4b90-a21f-54044574db30" timestamp="3.2" location="earth, the court" words="16167-16570">The lawyer argues that Varn's assignment puts her life at risk, and leaves the girl with no parent at home.</li>
<li id="c58e4b7f-b365-4c69-9b62-1001dc2d6fc9" timestamp="3.4" location="earth, the court" words="18656-19107">She puts the detective's photographs before the court: Varn with the politician, and the politician at the doors of the company.</li>
<li id="e6e5cb18-ddf3-4bbb-a06f-af763b38d43b" timestamp="3.5" location="earth, the court" words="20939-21351">When news of the alien ships around Mars reaches the court, the judge leans toward the father: the girl needs a parent who will come home.</li>
<li id="dfe42004-939c-483e-9583-148504e78d3f" timestamp="4.1" location="earth, the court" words="22390-22717">The judge announces that she will rule for the father.</li>
<li id="4a8327f1-a68b-4923-9884-de47f1b33e74" timestamp="4.5" location="earth, the court" words="26674-27129">On the day of the ruling, word comes that Varn's ship is lost. The politician asks the court for custody of her daughter, as Varn asked him to.</li>
<li id="145b23ba-cb74-4c19-bd9e-d9b934bc245f" timestamp="5.1" location="earth, the court" words="27174-27618">The detective's evidence shows that the father only wanted to hurt Varn. The court gives the girl to the politician, and the lawyer is left knowing whom she fought for.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="9b3935d3-7478-41e4-b6f1-b798fb29a439" title="Motherhood" -->

A mother's unconditional love for her child, shown as care. She protects the child from whatever threatens it now, and prepares it ahead of time by teaching it what it will need to live on its own and do well. At the limit, she puts the child's life above her own.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="b9c0b1c4-d7a0-43a0-9851-43b2f20d2377" title="Communication and understanding" -->

How beings exchange their understanding of the world through signs both sides can read. Without shared signs, each side reads the other's actions in its own terms, and the errors this produces grow into fear and harm.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="68586c5b-21cf-4b0a-bb5e-1dbdfffbb687" title="Conflict" -->

Misunderstanding made visible, in three forms:

- mental: the sides know they don't understand each other, but don't show it;
- verbal: the disagreement is put into words;
- physical: the disagreement turns into violence.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="79f782a4-37d2-41f8-8cd7-9060934c0c2f" title="War" -->

Conflict in its fullest form, mental, verbal and physical at once, between organized sides. It spreads violence and death across many people on both sides, including those who never wanted it and those who were never party to it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="444dbff0-6c83-46a1-9aa5-7e7de930cca5" title="Colonization" -->

Spreading one's influence and people over places that didn't belong to one's side, often by treating them as empty when they are not.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="e027ffff-3e82-463e-a6b6-a850b278e788" title="The traveller" -->

A living being stranded under the surface of Mars, taken for an artifact by those who find it. It watches them come, is hurt by their digging, and calls its people for help.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5871c182-b04e-49e8-bcd8-4fefc93b2567" title="The rescue fleet" -->

A fleet that comes for one of its own. To those who see it arrive it is silent and cannot be read; it answers no hail, and strikes whoever is hurting the one it came for.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c12a7d82-af4f-4856-be5d-97d59cd0c17c" title="The hostage" -->

A living being held by people who do not know it is alive. They call it the discovery, are ordered to protect it at all costs, and keep digging into it to learn about the fleet that has come for it, while its people wait in orbit.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="971b911f-ae76-4880-90c7-8c26dcdad430" title="The promise" -->

Varn's promise to talk to her daughter every day. Every call keeps it and carries the reader back to the daughter, until the last message keeps it one final time.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5d51efca-a011-4172-9392-afb0b8e6feac" title="The feared invasion" -->

The belief, growing in Varn, that the alien fleet will go on to Earth: from her doubt about the digging, through an order she cannot carry out and ships no one can read, to the certainty she gives her life for.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2ff08bb2-8566-426e-b0c9-fba88100c806" title="Corruption" -->

The use of public power for illegitimate private gain: an official who sells his influence over decisions to those who pay him, and serves them instead of the public.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5843ae96-e6ad-4522-94f8-c358c123d944" title="Sacrificing relationships for profit" -->

Putting gain, whether money, status or power, before the people one is close to: using them, or giving them up, for profit.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d2d12cb8-843f-4d96-af62-7071d89238b1" title="The need for partnership and closeness" -->

The need to be close to someone: warm, constant contact with another person, and knowing that the bond is stable, that the care is mutual, and that it will last. Without it, a person is lonely even among others.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="0d351cce-e027-4348-8306-c2d3ac27d526" title="Love" -->

A strong attraction and emotional attachment to another person: caring for them, wanting to be near them, and wanting their good.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="82b6924e-4633-4b1f-b63c-30349a4d1e69" title="Telling right from wrong" -->

Judging actions and character as right or wrong, and acting on that judgement, even when what is right goes against what pays.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="d09fe0ce-24f1-4f3d-a308-27eecce17fa2" title="Parenthood" -->

Raising a child: supporting their physical, emotional and social growth until they can stand on their own. It is made by care, not by blood: a parent is whoever does the raising.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="04b2985c-c874-499a-83d5-099204588536" title="Justice" -->

Treating people fairly, and giving each what is due to them. It is not the same as what the law decides: a court can follow the law and still be unjust.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5dfa71fe-7266-4f98-b74b-52295967e466" title="Revenge" -->

An act against a person in return for a grievance, real or imagined: wanting to hurt the one who hurt you.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="199cb3c7-d008-4fca-beeb-7e6abe365357" title="Varn as the means to an end" -->

To the politician, Captain Varn begins as a tool: a way into what the government knows about Mars, and leverage over the committee. He courts her coolly, sure that he will use her and move on.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="80ae4044-a324-457d-b4c6-0137bc5adcba" title="The company's hold on him" -->

The mining company that paid for the politician's career owns him: it gives him his orders, reminds him of what it knows about him, and drops him the moment he becomes a liability.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="6ea4919e-0356-4d1e-b62e-837c68207c4c" title="The pretence turning real" -->

The politician's affection for Varn starts as an act and becomes real without his noticing: from playing coy, through remote dates and talk of the future, to a love he recognizes only at the end.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="7664a45c-2ed3-4274-abea-5ac403b8f87d" title="The politician's fatherly instincts waking" -->

Looking after Varn's daughter wakes something in the politician he did not know was there: memories of his own childhood, a bond with the girl, and in the end a promise to look after her.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="f932cd6c-74fd-46d2-af52-6d01985deea2" title="The client's real motive" -->

Varn's ex-husband says he wants his daughter, but what he wants is to hurt Varn: he never asks about the girl, and the custody case is his way of making Varn pay.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="f965c372-a24f-49ea-a16d-fbfedd7f7de5" title="The paying client against the woman in the right" -->

The detective is paid to find dirt on Varn, and finds instead that she is in the right. Torn between the money and his conscience, he ends up on her side.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="c0177fa9-d44f-426d-a409-eda97bdab02a" title="The case against an absent mother" -->

In court, Varn is judged by her absence: her dangerous assignment is called neglect, her delayed answers from space are heard as evasions, and the man she keeps company with is held against her.

<!-- cell: markdown id="d5c3eaac-f4e4-41b3-af11-95a6dbb6dd7e" -->

The command center sat deep beneath the city, a long hall of tiered consoles washed in the blue glow of the great wall display. Across it, the red curve of Mars turned slowly beside the plotted lanes of the coming expedition, each projected course a thin line of light. Officers in pressed grey uniforms moved between the stations, speaking low into headsets, and the air carried the dry hum of machines that never rested. At the center of the floor, before the main plotting table, Captain Varn stood with her hands clasped behind her back, studying the escort formation she would command.

The politician came down the steps from the observation gallery with an aide at his elbow, his suit too fine for the room and his smile already in place. He dismissed the aide with a small gesture and crossed the floor alone, slowing as he reached the table so that he seemed to have arrived there by chance. He looked first at the display, then at her, and let his eyes linger as though he had only just noticed who she was.

"Captain Varn," he said, offering his hand. "I've read your record. I confess I came down here hoping to meet the woman behind it." She shook his hand briefly, her grip firm, her expression polite and guarded. He asked about the corvette, about the formation on the table, about the long months between here and Mars, and he listened to every answer with his head slightly tilted, nodding at the right moments, laughing softly when she allowed herself a dry remark about committees that had never flown a ship. When she pointed out a weakness in the planned approach, he leaned closer to the table, close enough that his sleeve nearly brushed hers, and told her that people in his position ought to hear more from people in hers.

Around them the officers went on with their work, and a few glanced over at the pair by the plotting table before turning back to their screens. Varn answered him evenly, giving nothing she did not mean to give. The politician kept his voice warm and his attention fixed on her, and as she turned back to the display, he watched her a moment longer than he needed to, his smile never slipping, his eyes measuring her the way the officers around them measured distances on the board.

When she finally straightened from the table and glanced at the clock above the wall display, the politician _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1d6ffa76-53b4-4551-bbbd-d4eef96091fe">was already reaching for his coat, a casual remark about a quiet little restaurant two streets away ready on his lips.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ea88b6e2-31b1-40a4-a744-59471cbe22e7" -->

The detective's office occupied the third floor above a shuttered noodle shop, and the smell of old broth still climbed the stairwell in the afternoons. Rain streaked the single window, blurring the neon of the street below into smears of red and green. Inside, a desk of scarred composite held a cold mug, a stack of unpaid invoices, and a cheap terminal whose screen flickered whenever the tram passed. Filing cabinets lined one wall, their drawers labeled in faded marker, and a coat stand in the corner leaned under the weight of a damp trench coat.

The man across the desk had not taken off his gloves. He sat forward on the edge of the client's chair, turning a wedding ring he no longer had any reason to wear around and around his finger. He laid a photograph on the desk: a woman in a navy officer's uniform, dark hair pinned back, caught mid-laugh beside a girl of about nine. "Captain Varn," he said. "My ex-wife. She's shipping out on the Mars escort in a few weeks. That's our daughter with her."

The detective picked up the photograph and studied it without comment. The client kept talking, faster now, his voice tightening. "She thinks she can leave the kid with a sitter and fly off to play hero for a year. I want custody. I want my daughter back, and I need something a judge can't ignore. Drinking, men, money, debts, I don't care what. Everyone has something. Find hers." He paused, and something raw passed over his face before he smoothed it away. "She was never as perfect as she let people believe."

The detective set the photograph down and slid a contract across the desk. "Daily rate plus expenses. I report what I find, not what you want me to find." The client signed without reading it, pressing hard enough that the stylus squeaked on the pad. He stood, glanced once more at the photograph of the woman laughing, and left it on the desk as he went out. The detective listened to his footsteps fade down the stairwell, then turned the picture toward the lamp and looked at it for a long while as the rain kept falling.

Over the following days the detective took to following Captain Varn at a careful distance, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="831ce51a-19e7-4614-8a50-c4de9de9fb67">noting the base gates she passed, the shops she lingered in, until one evening she stepped into a restaurant and he found a table by the window, where he could see who sat down across from her.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="b81fafa4-af6d-4330-8e2b-f4363b0d3504" -->

The restaurant sat on the forty-second floor of a tower near the river, and its windows ran from floor to ceiling, so that the city lay spread beneath the diners like a lit circuit board. Waiters in grey jackets moved between the tables without hurry. The light was low and amber, the linen heavy, the conversation kept to a murmur by the soft carpet and the distance between chairs. At a table by the glass, where Mars hung small and red above the skyline, the politician had already ordered wine when Captain Varn arrived in a plain dark dress that did not quite hide the bearing of a naval officer.

He rose to greet her, pulled out her chair, and asked about her week as if the answer mattered to him. She talked a little about the corvette's refit and the briefings at the agency. He listened with his chin on his hand, and between compliments he let small questions slip in. How many people would the survey team need? How long would they stay? Was the agency planning to dig, or only to look? She answered some and laughed off others, and each time she said something the committee had never been told, his eyes went still for half a second before he smiled again and refilled her glass.

Over the main course she set her fork down. "I should be honest with you," she said. "I have a daughter. She's eleven, she's the center of everything, and I'm about to leave her for months. I'm not looking for romance. I'm not looking for anything, really." He tilted his head, said that he understood perfectly, that he only enjoyed her company, and then asked her the girl's name in a voice so warm that Varn looked down at the tablecloth and smiled despite herself. He told her he would never dream of pressing, and he did not take his eyes off her.

Three tables away, half hidden behind a pillar and a menu he had not read, a man in a rumpled jacket nursed a glass of mineral water. He had followed Varn from her apartment, and he had expected to spend the evening watching her eat alone. Instead he studied her companion's face, the expensive cut of his suit, the practiced tilt of his head, until recognition came: he had seen that face on the news feeds, seated at the long table of the committee that oversaw the space agency. The detective lowered the menu a fraction, raised the small camera concealed in his palm, and took the picture.

When the dinner was over and the check had been settled, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="90af46c5-8935-484b-9e2d-abb68c62c8ab">the politician did not go home; he had his driver take him across town to the mining company's offices, where the men who had paid for every step of his career were waiting with the lights still on.</li>
<li edge="ea4fcbbf-0257-43f0-9c3f-2d2767eebfd7">he helped Captain Varn into her coat, said nothing of seeing her again, and let her be the one to ask when they might next share a table.</li>
<li edge="cb54ba22-e80c-4aef-a96c-ff1294cb90ba">the man at the corner table, who had nursed a single coffee all evening, lowered the camera hidden in his folded newspaper and watched Captain Varn rise from her seat across from the politician.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0ff39034-2bc1-4263-a6f1-c894186636d6" -->

The restaurant sat on the corner of a quiet street, its long front window fogged at the edges where the warmth inside met the evening chill. Lamps with amber shades hung low over the tables, and the waiters moved between them with practiced quiet, carrying plates and folded napkins, refilling water glasses before anyone thought to ask. Near the window, at the same table they had taken before, Captain Varn sat across from the politician. She wore civilian clothes that still held something of a uniform in their neatness, and her glass of wine stood barely touched beside her plate.

He leaned forward when she spoke and laughed a beat after she did, then sat back as if he had caught himself enjoying it too much. When the talk drifted toward anything that might sound like courtship, he turned it aside with a shrug and a joke about committee meetings, or let a compliment hang half-finished and asked for the bread instead. She told him about her daughter: a school project gone wrong, a drawing of a ship taped to the refrigerator, the way the girl had asked whether Mars had weather. Her face softened as she talked. He listened, nodding, and said that he would like to hear more about her next time, as though the next time were not yet certain.

Across the street, in a parked car with the engine off, a man in a dark coat raised a camera to the windshield. The shutter clicked softly, again and again: Varn lifting her glass and setting it down without drinking, the politician's hand resting near hers on the tablecloth and never quite reaching it, the two of them bent over a phone as she showed him a photograph. He lowered the camera, scrolled back through the frames, and frowned at them. There was nothing in them that anyone could use.

When the bill came, she paid her half. They stepped out together under the awning, and the politician offered to walk her somewhere; she shook her head, smiling, and said she had an early start. He watched her go with his hands in his pockets, then turned the other way. The man in the car took one last picture of her walking alone toward the corner, her collar turned up against the wind, until she rounded it and was gone. He set the camera on the passenger seat and sat for a while, looking at the empty street.

The politician walked on until the restaurant's amber lights had fallen far behind him, and at the next crossing he hailed a cab and gave the driver an address _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="3edfc3a6-7c5e-4032-99da-b82b12abfed6">on the far side of the river, where the company kept its offices on the top floors of a tower that showed no name at street level, and where, even at this hour, someone would be waiting for his report on Captain Varn.</li>
<li edge="b111f900-09ca-4f24-b8ed-6d338ee254bd">on the far side of the river, and did not notice the second cab that pulled away from the kerb behind his, where the detective sat watching his taillights through the rain.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="735054f0-e489-49bc-9262-f201e9f56ffc" -->

The space agency stood at the edge of the city like a town of its own, a sprawl of low white buildings and radar dishes laid out across flat, mown ground. Fences ran around it in double rows, and at the gates guards checked badges against screens while delivery vans idled in the morning heat. Above the main hall the agency's banner hung motionless in the still air, its emblem of a ringed planet faded at the edges by years of sun.

Inside, the mission control room was dim and cool, lit mostly by the glow of its consoles. Rows of desks descended toward a wall of screens where the red curve of Mars filled the largest display, its surface marked with a small grid of coordinates and the tiny icons of ships holding orbit above it. Technicians sat in their headsets, murmuring numbers to one another, their coffee cups going cold beside their keyboards. Every few minutes the delayed telemetry refreshed, and a column of figures shifted on the side screens: fuel reserves, hull temperatures, the slow descent of drilling rigs into rust-colored rock.

Along the upper gallery, behind a pane of glass, a cluster of officials in suits watched the floor below and spoke in low voices, pointing now and then at the screens. Down the corridors, engineers bent over printouts in crowded offices, and a team of scientists gathered around a table strewn with images from the dig site, arguing over the shape of something half-buried beneath the Martian dust. One of them traced its outline with a pen; another shook his head and pulled up a fresh scan.

The work went on without pause. Phones rang and were answered, doors opened and swung shut, and the soft chime of incoming transmissions sounded across the floor at intervals as regular as a heartbeat. On the great screen Mars turned slowly in the dark, and the people of the agency kept their eyes on it, measuring and recording everything that reached them across the long silence between the worlds.

On the great screen, the icons above the dig site shifted as a new transmission came in, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8c1e507b-7ce0-4319-a470-d7db8470c2ab">the first of the excavation landers settling into its descent toward the red plain where the artifact lay buried.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="150efda9-b3c0-41de-92d5-b83caf0eb0ba" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, its windows lit against the early dark. Inside, the living room was small and warm, cluttered in the way of a home where two people lived busy lives: a pair of school shoes kicked under the coffee table, a uniform jacket hung over the back of a chair with its captain's bars catching the lamplight, a half-finished puzzle of the solar system spread across a tray on the floor. The screen on the wall glowed blue and white, and the newscaster's voice filled the room.

Captain Varn sat on the couch with her boots off and her feet tucked beneath her, a mug of tea cooling in her hands. Her daughter had abandoned the puzzle to lean against her side, chin propped on her mother's shoulder, eyes fixed on the screen. The footage was grainy and slow: a rust-colored plain, a thin drill arm lowering from a probe's belly, then a false-color scan with a dark, regular shape buried beneath the red dust. "Analysts confirm," the newscaster said, "that the object lies several meters below the Martian surface, and that its structure does not match any known geological formation."

"Is it a spaceship?" the girl asked, sitting up straight.

Varn smiled and shook her head slowly, though her eyes did not leave the scan. "Nobody knows yet. Could be a rock that grew in a funny shape." She watched the image rotate, the smooth curve of it, the way it seemed to fold in on itself. On the screen, a panel of scientists began talking over one another, and the girl pressed closer, asking whether people would go there and dig it up, whether they could bring it home. Varn set her mug down on the table without drinking from it and put an arm around her daughter. "I expect someone will want to," she said quietly, and the two of them kept watching as the shape turned and turned in the blue light.

Two days later, Varn's tablet chimed on the kitchen counter, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="be9f3907-80db-4266-aeab-13658c114004">a summons from Command marked urgent, and she read it twice before looking across the kitchen at her daughter, still bent over her homework at the table.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e3d9ab2e-7b13-420d-81ea-09373cc10c57" -->

The offices of the mining company took up the top three floors of a tower that looked, from the street, like any other glass spire downtown. Inside, they did not look like a mining company at all. There were no core samples in display cases, no maps of seams and shafts, no hard hats hung by the door as ornaments. There was thick carpet the color of dried wine, wood paneling that had never seen a forest it was not bought from, and a long window behind the conference table that framed the city below like a possession. Men in dark suits stood by the doors and did not sit, and the receptionist had not asked the politician his name when he arrived.

The owners sat on the far side of the table, three of them, with the window at their backs so that their faces stayed half in shadow against the afternoon glare. The eldest had a heavy, patient face and hands folded on a closed folder. He did not offer the politician a drink, and the politician, who had been offered one every time before, noticed. On the wall screen beside them, frozen, was a grainy orbital image of a rust-red plain with a dark shape half-buried in it, ringed by the pale specks of survey markers.

"You've seen the reports," the eldest said. It was not a question. "They found something on Mars. Nobody knows what it is. Which means nobody knows what it's worth." He slid the folder across the polished wood, and it stopped a hand's width from the politician's fingers. "When people don't know what a thing is worth, they assume it's worth everything. In a month there will be twenty companies lining up for a piece of that dirt. We are not going to be one of twenty."

The politician opened the folder. Inside were draft terms for an exclusive mining concession, the committee's membership list with notes in the margins beside several names, and a page of figures he recognized as the sums that had paid for his last two campaigns. He closed it again without reading further. The youngest of the owners leaned forward into the light, smiling as if at an old friend. "We made you," he said pleasantly. "Every poster, every hall you spoke in, every vote you bought. Now you're going to get us Mars. All of it, and only us." Behind him the men by the door did not move, and the politician tucked the folder under his arm, nodded once, and said that he understood.

A week later, in a panelled committee room on the far side of the city, the politician rose with the folder's contents rewritten in his own careful hand and, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7b5149a2-d430-406d-87a6-a47ca5ccd7dc">smoothing his tie, turned to face the members seated along the long table, the company's bid lying open before him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="2fe75dd8-5379-4cfb-9846-00ca83e05555" -->

The landers came down on the plain at dawn, when the light over Mars was thin and the color of rust. Dust lifted around their struts and settled again with the slowness of a world that had never been in a hurry. Beyond them, half a kilometer off, the ground fell away into the pit the survey drones had marked: a raw wound in the regolith, its walls striped in ochre and black, and at its bottom, under a skin of frozen dust, a curve of something smooth that caught the morning and gave back no reflection at all.

The excavation team climbed out in pressure suits stenciled with the mining company's mark, their boots leaving the first prints the plain had ever held. They worked without much talk. Cables were run from the landers to the lip of the pit; anchors were driven into the rock with dull, airless thuds that the crew felt through their soles rather than heard. A surveyor knelt at the edge and swept a scanner across the depths, reading out figures in a flat voice over the shared channel, while the others set up floodlights along the rim and angled them down until the curve below shone like the shoulder of something asleep.

Then the robots were brought out. They were squat, many-legged machines, each the size of a ground car, their forelimbs fitted with drills and cutting heads and their bellies hung with sample bins. One by one the winches lowered them over the edge, swinging gently on their cables, their sensor lamps blinking awake as they descended. The crew watched on handheld screens as the first of them touched down, unfolded its legs, and steadied itself on the uneven floor of the pit.

At a word from the rim, the drills began to turn. Fine red dust boiled up into the floodlights and hung there, glittering, and the cutting heads bit into the frozen crust around the smooth surface, scraping it bare in long, patient strokes. On the screens the crew saw the dark curve widen as the machines cleared it, and someone on the channel let out a low whistle. The robots spread across the floor of the pit and went on digging, deeper, while above them the team stood at the edge and watched the dust rise.

By midday the robots had cut a trench all the way around the curve, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="86e0c370-b499-448c-b32d-cbba25ee73bf">and the dust they threw up drifted slowly back down over something pale and smooth that was beginning to show along the trench floor.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fa470c3f-9a28-4fe0-b95d-eb0140f14bc7" -->

The command center sat three levels beneath the ground, a long low hall lit by the blue wash of the tactical wall. Orbital tracks crawled across it in thin lines of light, and below them rows of officers bent over their consoles, murmuring into headsets, their coffee going cold beside their elbows. The air smelled of recycled heat and old carpet. At the far end, behind a pane of glass, the admiral's office glowed amber, and Captain Varn stood inside it with her cap tucked under her arm.

The admiral slid a slate across the desk toward her. On it was the outline of a corvette, lean and grey, and beneath it the orders: escort duty, the survey and excavation convoy to Mars, departure in nine days. "She's yours," the admiral said. "You asked for a ship. This is the one we have." Varn read the orders twice, her thumb resting on the corvette's name, and then she straightened and saluted. Her face gave nothing away, but when she stepped back out into the hall she stood a moment by the glass, watching the red dot of Mars hang in its corner of the tactical wall, before she went to find her daughter.

The girl was waiting on a bench by the lifts, swinging her feet, a visitor's badge clipped crooked to her jacket. A duty sergeant had given her a paper cup of cocoa, and she had drunk half of it and was picking at the rim. She looked up when her mother came, and something in Varn's walk made her stop swinging her feet.

Varn sat down beside her and took the cup, set it on the floor, and held both of the girl's hands. "They gave me a ship," she said. "A real one. I have to take it to Mars, with the people who are going to dig there." The girl asked how long, and Varn told her the truth: months, maybe longer. Around them the officers kept working, the lights of the wall kept moving, and someone laughed briefly at a console down the row. The girl pressed her forehead against her mother's shoulder and said nothing for a long while, and Varn did not hurry her, only rested her chin on the girl's hair and watched the little red dot on the wall until her daughter was ready to stand.

When they came home that evening, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="680cb49c-61ec-493c-b7b5-b0f7ae98966d">Varn's duffel bag was already packed and waiting by the front door, and her daughter would not let go of her hand.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="97509fde-982f-4293-82c0-c07303c43a26" -->

The dig site lay at the bottom of a shallow basin, where the rust-colored dust thinned to a crust over older, darker rock. Floodlights on telescoping masts threw hard white circles across the trench, and beyond their reach the plain ran flat and silent to a horizon that seemed too close. A pressurized shelter squatted at the trench's edge, its seams frosted, cables snaking from it to the drills and the slow-turning sieves. Inside their suits the excavation crew moved with the careful, heavy-footed patience of people who had learned not to hurry in thin air and low gravity.

It was a woman on the night shift who first called a halt. Her brush had scraped through the last of a compacted layer and met something that was not stone, or not any stone the survey had mapped. She knelt and swept again, and the floodlight caught a surface that drank the glare and gave back a faint, oily sheen, blue-black shot through with threads of silver. The others gathered at the lip of the trench, their helmet lamps converging, and someone on the open channel said only, "Well, look at that."

They worked through the rest of the shift by hand, trading the drills for brushes and dental picks, lifting the regolith away a few centimeters at a time. What emerged was a curve, then a ridge, then a long sweep of form that looked carved: a figure, or the suggestion of one, folded in on itself like a sleeper, its surfaces worn smooth in some places and finely grooved in others, as if a sculptor had left it half finished. The spectrometer, passed hand to hand, chattered out readings the technician read twice before speaking them aloud: alloys and lattices that had no business in Martian rock, elements so rare that a gram of them would have paid for the whole expedition.

By the time the small, pale sun cleared the rim of the basin, the object lay exposed to its shoulders, cold and dark against the red soil. The crew stood around it in a loose ring, saying little, while the cameras on the masts recorded every angle and the data streamed back toward the shelter's relay. One of them reached out a gloved hand and rested it flat on the smooth curve, the way someone might touch a statue in a museum when no one was watching, then drew it back and stepped away to let the surveyors mark out the next section of the dig.

Before the day shift had finished taking over the trench, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="068c1b1c-69c1-4f29-b25e-e8bf05869287">the first sealed sample cases had already been loaded onto the shuttle and were climbing out of the thin Martian air toward the laboratory ship waiting in orbit.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="41e38797-d2c1-42ad-b7f3-0748fd465043" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, its windows still grey with the early morning. Inside, the hallway was crowded with the small debris of leaving: a duffel bag stenciled with a fleet insignia, a pair of polished boots set neatly by the door, a uniform jacket draped over the banister where it had been hung the night before so it would not crease. In the kitchen, two mugs sat on the counter, one of coffee gone cold and one of cocoa barely touched. A drawing was pinned to the refrigerator by a magnet shaped like a planet: a lopsided red circle and, beside it, a small ship drawn in careful black lines.

Captain Varn knelt on the hallway rug so that her eyes were level with her daughter's. The girl was still in her pajamas, her hair flattened on one side from sleep, and she held the hem of her mother's shirt in one fist as if she had forgotten she was doing it. Varn smoothed the flattened hair with her palm, though it sprang back at once, and did it again anyway.

"It's a long way," the girl said. "Mars is a long way."

"It is," Varn said. "But the ship has a good transmitter, and I'll be the one in charge of it. So here is what's going to happen. Every day, I'm going to call you. Every single day. You'll tell me what you had for lunch and who was mean to you at school, and I'll tell you what Mars looks like out the window." She tapped the drawing on the refrigerator through the open kitchen doorway. "And you'll tell me if I got the color right."

The girl considered this with great seriousness. "Even when you're busy?"

"Especially when I'm busy. That's a promise." Varn held out her hand, little finger crooked, and after a moment her daughter hooked her own around it and squeezed hard. Then the girl let go of the shirt and threw both arms around her mother's neck, and Varn held her there on the rug for a long while, her eyes closed, while outside a car pulled up and idled at the curb and the light through the windows slowly turned from grey to gold.

Weeks later, through the forward viewport of the corvette's bridge, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e3150870-e21b-437c-b2fb-3ab3cc490da3">Captain Varn watched the rust-coloured disc of Mars swell slowly ahead, the laboratory ship's running lights holding steady off her starboard bow.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f72e58e2-5469-469c-aaee-7764713a4245" -->

The committee room sat deep in the old wing of the parliament, below the public galleries, where the windows had long ago been bricked over and replaced with screens. On the largest of them, a grainy image from Mars held still: a pit in the red ground, ringed with floodlights, and at its bottom a curve of something dark and smooth that caught the light the way no stone should. Around the long table the members sat beneath it in their shirtsleeves, coffee cooling at their elbows, clerks murmuring behind them and passing slips of paper forward.

He stood when his turn came, a folder open before him though he never looked at it. He spoke of jobs and of tonnage, of the cost of every day the site sat idle, of rival nations already drafting their own claims. The company he named had the equipment on the ground, he said, the crews trained for low gravity, the capital to move at once. An exclusive licence would put one hand on the work instead of a dozen, and one hand meant speed, order, accountability. He let his gaze travel the table as he said it, resting a moment on each face, a man who seemed to have already counted the votes.

The members did not meet him there. A woman at the far end tapped her pen against the image on the screen and asked what, precisely, they would be licensing: a mine, or a find. An older man beside her said that nobody in the room could tell him what that thing in the pit was made of, and he would not sign away a planet's worth of rights to dig into it blind. Others nodded. One asked for the survey reports; a clerk answered that they had not yet come back. The chair folded her hands and said, mildly, that the committee would revisit the bid once the scientists had told them what had been found, and not before.

He thanked them and sat down, closing the folder with care. Around him the talk had already moved on to the next item, the clerks shuffling papers, someone calling for fresh coffee. Above them the frozen picture from Mars stayed on the screen a while longer, the dark curve gleaming in its pit, until a technician switched it off.

That evening, when the committee had risen and the corridors had emptied, he took a car across the city to the tower where the company kept its offices, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="bab104ec-a35e-47a0-94e4-fa1b450bf3c6">where the owners were waiting for him on the top floor, eager to hear what use he meant to make of Captain Varn.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="4f82093e-c9bc-4855-97a9-da5f283da1d5" -->

Mars turned beneath them, rust and shadow, its thin atmosphere a pale rind along the curve of the horizon. The corvette came out of its braking burn with its drive still ticking as it cooled, a narrow grey hull scarred along the bow by years of patrol, and a few kilometres off its flank the laboratory ship settled into the same orbit, broad and slow, its sensor arrays unfolding like the petals of something cautious. On the corvette's bridge the crew worked through the arrival checklist in low voices, calling out station-keeping figures and the positions of the transports already parked above the dig site. Captain Varn stood behind the helm with one hand on the back of the pilot's chair and watched the planet fill the forward display.

Far below, at the edge of a long gouge in the red ground, a pinprick of light flared and climbed. The sample lifter rose on a column of exhaust, shrank to a spark, and arced up toward them. The comms officer read out its telemetry: three sealed canisters, cut from the object's surface that morning, inbound for the laboratory ship. Varn ordered the corvette to shadow the lifter's approach, and the bridge watched in silence as the little craft matched orbit and slid into the laboratory ship's open bay, the doors closing behind it like a mouth.

Inside the laboratory ship, the scientists were already waiting in the white glare of the receiving lab, gloved and masked, the canisters set down one by one in the isolation chambers behind thick glass. A technician cracked the first seal with remote manipulators. What lay inside was a curl of dark material no longer than a hand, smooth on one face and torn on the other, and under the lights its torn edge had a faint, wet sheen that none of them had expected from stone. Someone leaned closer to the glass. Someone else called for the spectrometer and the cutting laser, and the room filled with the hum of instruments warming up and the scratch of styluses on tablets as they began to take it apart.

On the corvette, the laboratory ship's first report came through: samples received, analysis under way. Varn read it twice, then looked back at the scar on the planet's face where the lifter had come from, and said nothing for a long moment before she ordered the crew to hold position and stand down to normal watch.

Far below, beneath the long gouge in the red ground, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ae990dc0-412d-4a61-b053-a7efedcbe994">the buried shape lay where it had lain for ages, and as the drills bit into the rock above it, something in the deep stone stirred to attention.</li>
<li edge="0ec1d0f9-3e65-4dff-aa9a-37705671ef4a">the dig crews worked on through the Martian night, while high above them, aboard the corvette holding its slow orbit, Captain Varn sat alone in her cabin and keyed in the code for home.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="b02ac810-443c-4f0a-882e-4d4038066541" -->

The offices of the mining company took up the top three floors of a glass tower above the river, and the lobby below was all black stone and brass, with a security desk that asked visitors their business twice. Rain streaked the windows. Across the street, under the awning of a closed noodle shop, a man in a grey coat lowered a camera with a long lens and checked the shot on its screen: the politician, collar turned up, stepping through the revolving doors and nodding to the guard as if he had done it many times before. The detective's client had told him to dig into this man, the one who kept turning up at Captain Varn's side, and he had followed him across the city on foot and by cab to reach this door. He looked up at the company's name in steel letters over the entrance, a name he knew from court reports and quiet rumors, and he took another picture.

High above, in a corner room paneled in dark wood, the owners sat around a long table with the river grey behind them. They were not men who raised their voices. One poured water for the others; another turned a pen slowly between his fingers while the politician spoke. He stood rather than sat, and he kept his hands open on the back of a chair.

"Varn will be my eyes on Mars," he told them. "Whatever that object is, whatever the survey finds before it reaches any report, she'll tell me. Through her I'll know more than anyone on the committee." The man with the pen said nothing, only looked at him, and the silence went on long enough that the politician's fingers tightened on the chair.

Then an assistant came in, bent to his ear, and set a printed sheet at his place. He read it standing. The orders had come through that morning: Captain Varn was to command the corvette escorting the mission to Mars. Something in his shoulders loosened, and he let out a breath he seemed not to know he had been holding. When he looked up again his voice had steadied, and he was already talking about what she might learn of the thing buried in the red ground, how close she would be, what questions he could put to her. The owners listened, and the man with the pen at last set it down on the table.

In the weeks after Captain Varn's corvette left Earth for Mars, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="012949a1-cc62-48b6-befe-e4e1c4aa2c5f">the politician made a habit of calling her every evening from his office, listening closely to everything she let slip about the dig in orbit above Mars, a notepad open at his elbow.</li>
<li edge="f25de376-eaec-469f-a60b-61e859174605">the politician took to staying late in his office after the committee had gone home, the door locked and the screen glowing as he waited for the light-delayed link to her ship in orbit to connect.</li>
<li edge="103a36ba-110e-4a00-b6c7-88104477181c">the detective spread his photographs of the politician and the company's offices across his desk, and waited for his client to arrive.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="96b722d2-d849-45e3-ac87-dc94ed9d2e03" -->

The basin lay under a thin rust-colored sky, its floor a crust of iron dust and fractured basalt that had not been disturbed in longer than anyone could reckon. At its center, half-buried in a shelf of dark rock, a curved surface caught the weak sunlight with a dull, pearly sheen. It was smooth where the stone around it was pitted, and faintly ridged, like the whorls on a fingertip. Fine grit had settled in those ridges over the ages, and none of it had ever moved. Beneath the surface, threaded deep into the rock in channels no wider than hairs, something had been patiently, slowly alive.

It had felt the probe long before the probe reached it. The small machine had come crawling across the basin on six jointed wheels, its sensor mast turning in slow arcs, and every vibration of its passage had travelled through the stone and into the waiting threads. When it stopped at the edge of the shelf and unfolded its scanning arm, a sweep of pale light passed over the ridged surface, back and forth, back and forth, and a low hum of instruments carried through the rock. Deep in its channels, the organism traced each pass. It mapped the shape of the machine by the tremors it made, measured the rhythm of the scan, and held still, the faint warmth in its fibers rising by a fraction of a degree as it attended.

Then the larger tremors came. A landing craft settled on the far rim of the basin in a long bloom of dust, and soon figures in white pressure suits were walking down the slope in a loose line, towing sledges of equipment behind them. They planted survey stakes in a ring around the shelf, unrolled cable, and set up a squat drilling rig on its tripod, their helmet lamps bobbing as they worked. One of them knelt and laid a gloved hand on the pearly curve, and another crouched beside the probe to read its display, gesturing toward the rock with obvious excitement. Their footsteps, the clank of the tripod legs, the thump of crates set down on the ground: all of it ran down into the stone, and the organism counted every step, learning the weight and number of the newcomers as they gathered around it.

The drilling rig's motor coughed once and settled into a steady whine, and deep within the rock the organism _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a0d5f4a7-6bc0-4c39-baed-c52acb26a702">felt the vibration travel through the stone toward it, faint at first, then nearer with every turn of the bit, until it could no longer tell the shudder of the drill from the slow beat of its own shell.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c581db02-2ef7-4260-a875-f3f0db2225d6" -->

The corvette hung above Mars with its running lights dimmed, the red planet filling half of every port on the starboard side. Below, a thin pale scar marked the dig site, and the watch crew moved quietly through the narrow corridors, checking gauges and logging the slow turn of the orbit. In the small cabin behind the bridge, Captain Varn sat with her jacket unbuttoned and two windows open on the console, each of them waiting out the long minutes the signal took to cross from Earth.

The first window filled with her daughter's face, close to the camera, hair still wet from a bath. "I miss you," the girl said, and then said nothing else, as if it had taken all her nerve to get that much out. Varn watched the words arrive, touched the edge of the screen with two fingers, and leaned toward the microphone. "I miss you too, sweetheart. Every single day up here." She sent it, and sat very still while it crossed the dark, her eyes on the frozen picture of the small face.

In the other window the politician waited, his tie loosened, a glass of water at his elbow, the city lights of an Earth evening behind him. He had set the call for the hour he called their dinner, and he raised his glass to the camera when her answer reached him. She told him the object below was unlike any rock the geologists had ever cut into, that the scans came back strange and changed between passes, and that the agency was keeping every finding quiet, even from the crew. When her words had come through, he nodded gravely, said that it must weigh on her, and while he spoke his other hand moved off the edge of the frame, over a second screen, tapping out a short message. He sent it before he looked back up at her.

Then the talk drifted, as it had begun to on these calls, away from Mars. He asked what she would do when the tour was over, and she laughed and said she had never let herself plan that far. He said she ought to, that there would be a house somewhere with a yard for her daughter, and long dinners that did not need a twenty-minute delay between bites. Varn smiled at the screen, glanced once at her daughter's frozen face in the other window, and said that maybe, when she came home, they could talk about it properly. Outside the port, the scar on the red surface slid slowly out of sight.

In the days that followed, while the corvette kept its slow watch above the pale scar, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="349dabc1-77c0-449a-9e5e-911211da5275">Captain Varn spent long hours at the observation port, the line to Earth open beside her, watching the excavators' lights sink deeper into the red dust.</li>
<li edge="477ea5f0-3a6b-46da-a336-c15a53fe2e82">the politician carried every doubt she had confided to him across the void and into the committee room, where the men who decided how deep Mars would be cut sat waiting for his words.</li>
<li edge="75031a43-cfbb-4833-ab12-1301068ca59c">the politician's calls came to Varn's bridge more often, and their talk began to drift from the dig below toward what might come after, and toward the girl she had left behind on Earth.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0e67c276-911e-4d66-bfa9-7c8beeb18b6a" -->

The detective's office sat above a laundromat, and the warmth of the machines rose through the floorboards so that the room always smelled faintly of soap and hot lint. Rain ran down the single window in crooked lines, blurring the neon of the street below into smears of red and green. A desk lamp threw a hard circle of light across the blotter, where a manila envelope lay squared to the edge, and beyond that circle the filing cabinets and the coat rack stood in shadow.

The client sat across the desk in a good coat he had not bothered to take off. The detective slid the envelope over to him and watched him tip the photographs out: the politician at a café table with Captain Varn, the politician leaning close to say something that made her laugh, the politician alone on the steps of a glass-fronted building downtown. On a slip of paper clipped to the last print, the detective had written the name of the company that owned that building, and beneath it, in smaller letters, the families it was said to answer to.

The client read the name twice. Then he smiled, slowly, the way a man smiles at a hand of cards he did not expect to be dealt. "That's good," he said. "That's very good. A man like that, around her. Around a mining outfit with friends like those." He fanned the photographs out across the blotter and tapped the one of Varn laughing. "She'll hate this. She'll absolutely hate it." He asked whether there were more prints, whether the negatives were safe, whether the politician had been seen at her home, and how soon the detective could get him something with dates on it.

The detective answered each question in a flat, even voice and waited for the other one, the one fathers usually asked sooner or later, about the girl, about whether she had been seen with the man, whether she had seemed all right. It did not come. The client gathered the photographs back into the envelope with careful, almost tender hands, tucked it inside his coat, and stood. When the door had shut behind him and his footsteps had faded down the stairwell, the detective sat for a long while in the circle of lamplight, listening to the rain and the dull churn of the machines below, and found that he did not like the feeling in his chest at all.

After a while he pulled a fresh notepad from the drawer and, instead of the captain's name, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="155f4eae-86a0-433b-bff2-f7b0bc21869d">wrote the name of the man who was paying him at the top of the first page.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="9c33ff53-5b7b-49b6-9bf2-293f1916097c" -->

The basin lay under a thin pink sky, its floor scoured smooth by winds older than any human word. In the middle of it, half-buried in rust-coloured dust, rose the thing the surveyors had called the artifact: a long, ribbed curve of dark material, too regular to be stone, too seamless to be metal. Floodlights on tall masts ringed it, and between them crawled the excavators, broad-tracked machines with drills and scoops folded against their flanks like the claws of sleeping crabs. Prefabricated domes squatted at the edge of the site, and from their airlocks came the crew in pressure suits, trailing tethers and cables, their voices crackling over the open channel as they called out depths and angles.

The first cuts went into the soil around the curve. Scoops bit, lifted, and swung away; dust rose in slow plumes and settled again on visors and instrument panels. Deep beneath the ribbed surface, something stirred. A faint warmth spread through the buried mass, unnoticed by the thermal probes calibrated for rock and ice. Each vibration of the tracks, each shudder of a scoop striking the ground, reached it as a pressure it had not felt in ages, and it drew itself inward. Along the exposed ridges, tiny plates shifted by fractions of a millimetre. A geologist kneeling with a hand scanner frowned at a reading, tapped the screen, and shrugged when the numbers settled back.

By the second shift the drills came down. The lead excavator positioned itself over the widest rib, and its operator, lit green by the glow of the cab displays, lowered the bit until it touched the dark surface. The drill whined, slipped, and found purchase. The crew on the channel cheered when the depth counter began to climb: the casing was harder than anything they had cut before, and every centimetre was a small victory. Then the bit punched through. A thin jet of pale fluid sprayed up around the shaft, freezing almost at once into glittering threads that hung in the low gravity before drifting down. Someone swore. The operator froze the drill.

Beneath the plain, the wounded thing convulsed. The ground trembled, not enough to topple the masts, but enough to send the crew staggering and the floodlights swaying in long arcs across the dust. And from the ribbed curve, from the whole buried length of it, a signal went out: not sound, for the air was too thin to carry it, but a pulse that flooded every receiver at the site with a howl of static, spiked every instrument off its scale, and lanced upward through the pink sky, past the moons and the dark beyond them, toward something far away that would hear it and answer.

Far beyond the reach of the human receivers, the pulse was heard, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="645bc048-2064-4d91-964f-1287cf09cfe5">and an answer came racing back across the dark, faster than light, to the wounded thing that lay buried beneath the red dust of Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="6ee7c18b-c885-4b9b-a817-2ecb7a2e8145" -->

The corvette hung in high orbit above Mars, its hull turning slowly against the rust-colored curve of the planet. On the bridge the lights were dimmed to the blue of the night watch, and the crew worked quietly at their consoles, murmuring coordinates and fuel figures to one another. Captain Varn stood at the forward display, where the long-range optics showed the excavation site far below: a pale scar in the red dust, ringed by floodlamps, the great machines crawling in and out of a pit that had been half as deep only a week before. At its heart lay the dark curve of the object, still mostly buried, its surface swallowing the light the lamps threw on it.

Two windows glowed on the console beside her. In one, her daughter sat cross-legged on a bed back on Earth, hair still wet from a bath. In the other, the politician leaned toward his camera in a shirt with the collar undone, a glass of water at his elbow. Every word took minutes to cross the dark, and the three of them had learned to talk in long, patient turns, letting each message arrive before answering it.

"They went down another forty meters today," Varn told her daughter, her eyes still on the pit. "Nobody down there knows what it is. Not the geologists, not the engineers, not the people who sent them. And they keep digging anyway." She was quiet for a moment, watching an excavator tilt its arm toward the black surface. "Sometimes I think that's the part that scares me most." When she turned back, her voice softened. "Brush your hair before you sleep, all right? I'll call again tomorrow."

Then she faced the other window, and her shoulders dropped a little, as if she had set something down. "I need to ask you something," she said to the politician. "She's on her own more than I'd like right now. Would you look in on her? Just see that she's all right, that she's eating, that someone's there." She waited through the long silence of the delay, her hand resting on the edge of the console, until his answer reached the ship. He nodded on the screen before he spoke. "Of course," he said. "I'll go by this week. I'll make sure she's all right." Varn let out a breath and thanked him, and below them the floodlamps burned on over the deepening pit.

Back on Earth, in the quiet of her mother's empty house, the girl _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="ed793d2c-da3b-4c3d-9a84-8589b810cdb3">sat cross-legged before the screen, waiting for the delayed link to Mars to open and her mother's face to appear.</li>
<li edge="9cc74a17-ad4d-4ab9-859e-485620632ebc">heard the doorbell ring and ran to answer it, hoping it would be the man her mother had told her about, the one who had promised to look in on her.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="30c1f797-bce5-4d30-998b-7906305fff44" -->

Rain had been running down the window of the detective's office since noon, blurring the neon of the noodle bar across the street into long red smears. Inside, the air smelled of cold coffee and printer toner. A desk lamp threw a hard circle of light over a scatter of photographs: a woman in a fleet uniform crossing a parade ground, the same woman at a school gate with a girl's hand in hers, the same woman laughing at a café table across from a man in an expensive coat. The detective had spread them out that morning to choose which ones to send. He had not chosen any.

He sat back in the creaking chair and looked instead at the folder on the corner of the desk, the client's folder, thin and almost empty: a name, an address, a retainer paid in full and in advance. Beside it lay a printout of the client's last message, and the detective read it again. *Anything. I don't care how small. If she so much as raised her voice at the kid, I want it.* Below it, after the photographs of the café, the client had written only, *Good. More of this.* The detective tapped the word *Good* with one finger, slowly, as if testing whether it would give.

Then he pushed the photographs of the woman into a neat stack, turned them face down, and set them aside. He pulled the keyboard toward him. On the screen, the search fields that had held her name for three weeks went blank, and he typed the client's name in its place. Court records first: a divorce, years old, and a string of filings after it, motions and appeals that had gone nowhere. Then property records, then old employers, then a news archive. He worked without hurry, copying each line into a new file, pausing now and then to rub his eyes or take a mouthful of coffee that had long gone cold.

Near midnight he stopped and opened a fresh folder on the desk, a clean manila one, and wrote the client's name across the tab in black marker. He slid the first printed page inside it, laid it squarely in the circle of lamplight where the photographs had been, and sat looking at it while the rain went on against the glass.

Two days later the detective sat behind the wheel of his car, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="b883d1f3-9a7a-493f-b640-8912f8c26cd0">parked across the street from Captain Varn's house, his camera on the passenger seat as the politician's car pulled into her drive.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="54617b39-445b-4925-ac7a-78009257091b" -->

The lawyer's office occupied a corner of the forty-first floor, and the late afternoon came through its two walls of glass in long amber bars that lay across the carpet and the broad walnut desk. The city spread below, its towers catching the light, and somewhere above the haze, too faint to see, Mars hung in the sky where the excavation fleet had gone. The room smelled of coffee and new leather. Shelves of bound case law lined the inner wall, more for show than use, and a single framed diploma hung behind the desk where a client could not miss it.

The man sat forward in the client's chair with a manila envelope on his knees, and he did not wait to be asked before opening it. He laid the photographs out one by one on the walnut, squaring each against the last: Captain Varn in her dress uniform at a restaurant table, laughing; a well-dressed man leaning close to her across a candle; the same man on the steps of a glass-fronted building whose brass lettering named a mining company. In another, the man shook hands with two heavy-set figures beside a black car. The ex-husband tapped that one twice with his finger. "My detective took these," he said. "She's sitting up there on a warship, and down here she's keeping company with a man who keeps company with them. And my daughter is living with whoever she left her with."

The lawyer let him talk. She was a composed woman in a charcoal suit, and she picked up each photograph by its edges, turned it toward the window, and set it down again in an order of her own. She asked when the pictures had been taken, how long the captain's mission would last, whether the girl had ever been left in the politician's care. He answered quickly, sometimes before she had finished, and twice he smiled at things that were not funny. She noticed, and wrote nothing about it.

At last she squared the photographs into a single stack and slid them back into the envelope. "A mother on a long, dangerous posting off-world," she said, "and an association like this one. A court will listen to that." She drew a retainer agreement from her drawer, turned it to face him, and laid a pen across it. He signed without reading past the first page, pressing hard enough that the nib scratched, and pushed it back to her. She countersigned, stood, and offered her hand across the desk, and he shook it with the satisfied grip of a man who believed he had already won.

When the morning of the first hearing came, the lawyer _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="baea2afe-a380-488b-8a6c-fe480a595662">arrived at the court early, the detective's photographs sorted in a folder under his arm, and took his place at the father's side to wait for the judge.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="92acf43b-46a8-4122-8db9-02ec2eace7d7" -->

The committee room sat deep in the old parliament building, below the public galleries, where the windows had long since been bricked over and the light came from long panels set into a ceiling of stained oak. A horseshoe of desks faced a single lectern. Clerks moved along the walls with tablets and water jugs, and behind the members their aides leaned in to whisper and leaned out again. On the wall above the chair hung a screen showing the excavation site on Mars: a rust-colored trench cut around a curved, dark surface that caught the floodlights strangely, as if it were wet.

The politician stood at the lectern without notes. He waited until the murmuring stopped, then spoke in an easy voice. He said the escort command reported the dig was going too slowly. The crews were working with hand tools and light borers, he said, and every week they spent scratching at the surface left the site exposed and the escort stretched thin, far from home. He named the hours the corvette spent holding station, the strain on its people, the fuel. He knew these things in more detail than anyone else in the room, and the members could hear it. Several of them glanced at one another.

"I won't pretend to speak for the military," he said, "but I've spoken with the people out there. They don't want to stay any longer than they have to. Neither should we." A member near the end of the horseshoe asked where heavier equipment would come from on short notice. The politician had the answer ready. A mining firm already had deep-bore rigs and plasma cutters certified for Martian conditions, he said, crated and waiting at the orbital yards. They could launch within the month. The terms were on the members' tablets. Pages turned on a dozen screens at once.

The chair called the vote by show of hands. The politician did not look at the count. He looked at the screen above the chair, at the dark curve in the trench, while the clerk read the figures aloud: the motion carried, and the committee ordered the excavation sped up and deepened, using the company's machines. Aides were already typing before the gavel came down. The politician collected his jacket from the back of his chair, shook two hands on his way to the door, and stepped out into the corridor, where his phone had begun to buzz in his pocket.

He had gone only a few steps along the corridor before he heard his name called behind him, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8f48dd3e-34f8-4e51-a872-627347c11a3e">and turned to find a knot of committee members hurrying to catch him up, each of them wanting to know what the agency had found on Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="ecde6dc2-a858-4a4e-8841-98b4cab0c6fa" -->

Wind moved the red dust across the floor of the crater in long, low sheets, and the floodlights of the dig cut white circles through it. In the center of the pit, half freed from the rock, lay the thing the crews called the artifact: a dark, curving mass, smooth as river stone and veined faintly with something that was not quite metal. Scaffolds ringed it. Drill rigs leaned against its flanks like insects at a wound. Men and women in pressure suits worked in shifts around it, cutting deeper channels along its sides, lifting away cores of stone and, now and then, thin brittle flakes of the thing itself, which they sealed into sample cases and carried up the ramps to the waiting crawlers.

None of them noticed when the veins beneath the surface began to glow. It was a slow, dim pulse, deep within the mass, like a coal breathed on. The drills kept turning. A cutting bar bit into the curve near the base, and the pulse quickened, and somewhere under the crust of Mars a signal went out that no instrument on the surface was built to hear, faster than light, out past the orbits of the planets and into the dark.

The answer came almost at once, from very far away. A presence turned its attention toward him, calm and measured, and asked what the nature of his emergency was.

He had been alone and asleep in the rock for longer than the planet had held its thin air, and his reply came slowly, in pain. He had been discovered, he told them. Small creatures had come down from the sky and found him, and they were cutting into him, and they were taking pieces of him away. He was being attacked. Above him a crane swung a fresh load of stone over the lip of the pit, and a crew chief waved the next rig forward. The distant voice was steady. He was to stay where he was, it told him, and not to move. Help was on the way. In the crater the veins dimmed again to nothing, and the drills went on turning in the floodlights while the dust blew across the dig.

Far above the crater, beyond the thin haze of the Martian sky, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="baf5d260-62b0-48b8-91d6-2fc57dce264c">the stars on the far side of the planet's orbit had begun to shimmer and bend, and aboard the escort ships the long-range sensors were already turning toward them.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="4d5c9db8-5c1d-4315-8a28-4969ddc9a189" -->

The house stood at the end of a quiet street, narrow and white, with a porch swing that creaked in the wind and a kitchen window full of afternoon light. Inside, the walls were crowded with photographs of Captain Varn: in dress uniform at some ceremony, laughing on a beach with a small girl on her shoulders, asleep in an armchair with a book open on her chest. The politician had arrived that morning with groceries he did not quite know how to use, and by noon he had burned one grilled cheese, rescued a second, and been told firmly by the girl that the crusts were supposed to stay on.

In the late afternoon the screen on the kitchen counter chimed, and Varn's face appeared, grainy and tired, the corvette's grey bulkhead behind her. The girl climbed onto a stool and pressed close. "Is the thing on Mars dangerous?" she asked, and then waited through the long silence while her words crossed the dark. When the answer came, Varn was smiling. "Don't you worry about it, sweetheart. It's just a rock that people are very excited about. I'm keeping an eye on everyone." The girl nodded, satisfied, and began telling her mother about the burned sandwich. The politician stood back by the sink and said nothing.

Afterward the girl led him from frame to frame, telling him a story for each: how her mother had taught her to whistle through a blade of grass, how she always sang the wrong words to songs on purpose, how she had once driven all night just to be home for a school play. Before one picture, a woman in uniform kneeling to tie a child's shoe at a crowded spaceport gate, the politician stopped. He looked at it longer than at the others, his hand drifting to his own jacket as if to straighten it, and when the girl asked what was wrong he only said that someone had once tied his shoes like that, before leaving for a long time. Then he let her drag him out to the porch swing, where she fell asleep against his arm while he read to her from a book about whales.

Across the street, in a parked sedan with the engine off, a man with a camera on his knees had watched it all through the kitchen window and then the porch rails. He had come expecting to catch the politician making calls, meeting someone, slipping up. Instead he had a memory card full of sandwiches, picture frames and a sleeping child, and a man on a swing who did not move for an hour so as not to wake her. The detective lowered the camera, rubbed his eyes, and kept watching long after the light had gone.

The following evening, a long way from that quiet street, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fe5e35f6-7834-45d5-8f7d-9b6aaba94004">Captain Varn sat on the bridge of her corvette in its slow orbit of Mars, watching the long-range screens out of habit while the night watch dragged on around her.</li>
<li edge="495e4e72-9a07-4e10-8a94-24aef9132427">the corvette hung in orbit above the rust-red curve of Mars, and on her bridge Captain Varn waited for the politician's call to come through.</li>
<li edge="fceb5632-5ffe-4499-929b-638eba75e5f8">the detective sat alone in his office with the lamp still burning, a stack of photographs of the politician and the girl spread across his desk and a blank message to Captain Varn's ship open on the screen before him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="8876bdef-31e9-483c-a08f-d222359aa0df" -->

The family court sat on the fourth floor of an old municipal building, in a room panelled with wood that had gone dark with age and long afternoons. Rain ran down the tall windows, and the light that came through them was grey and thin. A clerk sorted papers at a side desk, a court reporter waited with her hands resting on her machine, and the judge sat high behind the bench, reading glasses pushed down her nose. At one table the father sat very straight in a pressed suit, his hands folded, his eyes fixed on the bench. The table across from him was empty.

The lawyer rose and buttoned his jacket. He was a lean man with a careful voice, and he did not hurry. He laid the petition before the court: full custody of the girl, to be given to her father. The mother, he said, was not in the room because she was not on the planet. She had accepted command of a military corvette escorting the excavation on Mars, and had left her daughter behind for a posting that could last months and might not end at all. "No one doubts her courage," he said, and let the words sit a moment. "But a child cannot be raised by courage from a hundred million kilometres away. A child needs a parent who will come home."

Then he took a folder from his briefcase and handed copies to the clerk, who passed one up to the bench. Photographs, long-lensed and grainy: the mother at a restaurant table, the mother walking beside a well-dressed man on a lit street at night. The lawyer named him as a sitting politician, and named the mining company he served, a company whose ties to organised crime, he said, were a matter the court could weigh for itself. "This is the company she keeps," he said. "And this is the company her daughter would keep."

The judge turned the photographs over one by one, saying nothing. The father did not look at them. He kept his gaze on the bench, and something at the corner of his mouth tightened, almost a smile, before he smoothed it away. The rain went on against the windows, and the reporter's keys clicked softly as the lawyer asked that the mother be summoned to answer, wherever she was.

The judge set the photographs down, squared their edges against the bench, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="6707810e-3776-4402-b17f-f2d07b04a8c3">turned toward the screen at the side of the courtroom, where Captain Varn waited on the bridge of her corvette, millions of kilometres away, for a question she would not hear for several minutes.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="89f44339-fea9-4beb-ba52-d7ee6f706b72" -->

The members' lobby of the parliament was loud with the low, hurried noise of a chamber waiting on a vote. Rain streaked the tall windows, and the portraits of long-dead speakers looked down on clerks carrying folders and members talking in twos and threes beneath the vaulted ceiling. Screens along the far wall showed the committee's agenda in pale letters. Near the top sat the matter of the Mars licence: exclusive rights of excavation for a single mining company, at a depth and pace the agency had not yet approved.

The politician stood by a pillar near the tea urns, in a grey suit, his hands empty, as if he had nowhere in particular to be. He did not go looking for anyone; they came to him. First came a member from the agriculture benches, frowning over a printout. "The agency won't release the survey data," she said. "Not before the vote. They say it's under review." He nodded, lowered his voice, and told her what the mission had found at the site: how stable the ground was, how slowly the current teams were working, how much was being left untouched beneath the surface. She wrote quickly in the margin of her printout. When she looked up, her frown was gone.

Others followed through the next hour. An older member with a cane asked whether the object was dangerous. A younger one, sharp and suspicious, wanted to know where his figures came from. To each he gave the same calm account, precise and assured, the details of someone who had heard them firsthand from the people on the ground. "The escort's own reports," he said once, and left it at that. Members went back to their benches and whispered to their neighbours, and the whispers spread along the rows.

When the bell rang and the division was called, the members filed through the lobbies under the clerks' eyes. The politician walked through with them and did not look at the tally board until the count was read out. The licence had its majority, comfortably, with votes to spare from benches that had been undecided that morning. Around him there was scattered applause and a few sour looks. He accepted a handshake from a member he barely knew, straightened his cuffs, and walked out into the corridor, where the rain still beat against the glass.

He did not go back to his office but took the stairs down to the street, where _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fd70ff4f-22b1-4d6f-90e6-104971076b73">a car from the company was already idling at the curb, its rear door held open, ready to take him across the city to the men who owned him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="556cd3eb-d02f-4bde-b56d-115d1e115333" -->

Mars hung below the corvette like a dull coin, its rust plains scored by the pale scar of the dig site. The bridge was dim and close, lit mostly by the green and amber of the consoles, and the crew sat at their stations in the low murmur of routine: a helmsman trimming the orbit, a sensor officer sipping cold coffee, a comms tech logging the chatter of the survey ships strung out in their own slow circles around the planet.

The alarm came first from the other Earth ships, a burst of overlapping traffic on the fleet channel, and a heartbeat later the corvette's own sensors caught up. The sensor officer set down her cup without looking at it. Far out, on a high orbit beyond the outermost moon, space had seemed to fold, and shapes had slid out of the fold one after another: long, dark, unlit hulls that matched nothing in any registry. The tactical display filled with new markers, each tagged unknown, each drifting with a calm that no wreck or rock possessed.

Captain Varn stood at the center of the bridge with her hands on the rail and watched them bloom across her screens. She counted them silently, lips barely moving, and when the count passed twenty she stopped. "Hold position," she said quietly. "All stations, full sensor sweep. Nobody transmits to them until I say so." The crew bent to their work, and the bridge went very still except for the soft chime of contacts still appearing.

The private channel lit at her elbow. She almost let it ring, then touched it, and his face flickered onto the small screen, lagged and grainy across the distance. He did not begin with his usual easy charm. He looked at her for a long moment as though seeing her for the first time, and when he finally spoke his voice was unsteady, softer than she had ever heard it. "I just wanted to hear you," he said, and swallowed, and seemed surprised at himself. Behind her, on the main display, the dark ships kept coming.

Somewhere far from that dim bridge, the people who gave the orders _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="43cfa2dc-1c49-4629-918c-9e6c3c4d39ca">were already drafting new instructions, and the signal they would send was meant for the two ships still circling Mars, the laboratory vessel and its lone, lightly armed escort.</li>
<li edge="de9fc47e-0ec5-4f0e-aa5d-860d0a8c1f5c">were already drafting the words that would reach her across the dark, while Captain Varn stood at the viewport and watched the lights of the arriving fleet multiply above Mars.</li>
<li edge="25d9cba0-e7c2-4d5e-80c0-bbc58719ee79">were gathering in the company's offices on Earth, where the politician had been summoned to hear what they wanted next from Captain Varn.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="71fe3cec-59ba-4cee-bb96-1a937a99e6fb" -->

The detective's office sat above a laundromat, and the warmth of the dryers rose through the floorboards so that the room never quite cooled, even at night. Rain ran down the single window in crooked lines, smearing the neon of the street below into pink and green. The desk was too big for the room. Across it lay prints from his camera in loose rows: a woman in a navy officer's coat on the steps of a government building; the same woman laughing at a café table across from a man in a good suit; a girl of about nine on a park swing while that same man, coat off and sleeves rolled, pushed her higher than she had asked to go.

He had looked at that last one for a long while. The man in it was not watching the camera, or the street, or his phone. He was watching the girl, and the girl had turned her head mid-swing to shout something back at him, and both of them were plainly laughing. The detective set it face down, then turned it over again.

He pulled the keyboard toward him and opened the relay service, the one that queued civilian messages for the ships out at Mars. The cursor blinked in the empty field. He typed three lines, deleted them, and lit a cigarette he did not smoke. In the end he wrote only what he could stand behind: *Captain Varn. You don't know me. I've been doing some work that concerns you and your daughter, and I think you should hear about it from me before you hear about it from anyone else. When you're able to, I'd like to talk. Any time, any link. I'll make myself available.* He added the number for the office and the one for his own phone, and no name for the man who was paying him.

His finger rested over the key. Somewhere far out past the rain the message would wait its turn in the queue, cross the dark in minutes that felt like hours, and arrive on a ship he had only ever seen in news footage. He pressed send. The screen showed *Queued for transmission*, then *Sent*, and he sat back in the creaking chair with the photographs spread before him, listening to the dryers turn beneath the floor.

When no answer had come by the third morning, the detective put on his coat and went out into the wet streets, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="7e0626ca-cb2b-4048-9e01-85d07c58095a">heading for the school where Varn's daughter spent her days, to ask the people who saw the girl every morning what kind of mother had been raising her.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="29cf2fb5-23b1-4a30-8111-49f4f802c376" -->

The family court sat on the fourth floor of an old municipal building, in a room panelled in pale wood that had been varnished too many times. Rain streaked the tall windows. Before the bench, where a witness box might once have stood, a wide screen had been mounted on a steel frame, and on it, grainy and faintly green, was the bridge of a corvette in orbit around Mars. Captain Varn sat at its centre in her duty uniform, her hair pinned back, her hands folded on the arm of her chair. Behind her, crew moved between consoles, their voices a low murmur the microphones barely caught. In the lower corner of the screen a small white counter read the signal delay: eleven minutes, fourteen seconds.

The father's lawyer stood and asked his question cleanly, facing the screen as though she could see him at that moment. Did the captain consider a posting of indefinite length, on a mission she could not describe in open court, compatible with the daily care of a twelve-year-old girl? Then he sat down, and the room waited. The judge turned a page. A clerk tapped at a terminal. On the screen, Varn, still answering something asked a quarter of an hour before, said that yes, her sister had keys to the apartment, and that the school had been informed. The lawyer let a small, patient smile cross his face and glanced toward the bench. The father, at the table beside him, did not look at the screen at all.

The minutes dragged. A man in the back row coughed. Rain drummed on the glass. When at last the question reached her, the room saw Varn hear it: her jaw set, and she drew a breath before she spoke. She said that her orders came from the Fleet, that she had not chosen the timing, and that she would answer anything about her daughter that the court wished to know, but not about the mission, because she was not permitted to. Before she had finished, the lawyer was already on his feet with the next question, and the judge, frowning at the counter in the corner of the screen, allowed it.

So it went through the afternoon. Each answer arrived late and out of step, landing after the lawyer had moved on, so that it seemed to answer nothing, or to answer something else. When Varn hesitated, the hesitation reached the court eleven minutes later and hung there in silence. When she was precise, she sounded rehearsed. The judge wrote in short strokes and looked up at the screen less and less. Near four o'clock, Varn leaned toward the camera and said, quietly, that she would like it noted that she loved her daughter, and that she was sorry the distance made this difficult. By the time her words reached the room, the clerk was gathering papers, the lawyer was buttoning his jacket, and the judge was announcing the date of the next hearing.

At the next hearing, when the lawyer rose again, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="750b8823-44dd-4838-901d-a782ca0568bc">he turned to face the screen where Captain Varn waited, her image frozen for a moment by the delay from Mars, and he squared the papers in his hands.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="515f900c-1c9c-40ce-85a5-a0e1613473cd" -->

The company's offices took up the top floors of a glass tower that looked out over the city, and the boardroom at its heart had no windows at all. The walls were panelled in dark wood, the carpet swallowed every footstep, and a long table of black stone ran the length of the room beneath a single strip of cold light. On a screen at the far end, a slow feed from Mars turned over and over: red dust, the scaffolds of the dig, and in the middle of it the curved grey flank of the object, half free of the ground. The politician sat at the near end of the table with his hands folded on a closed folder. Across from him sat the owners, and beside them the men who oversaw him, who did not open folders and did not take notes.

One of the owners nodded at the screen. "Your captain's corvette is holding too far out," he said. "We want her closer. We want her people looking at that thing up close, scanning it, touching it if they have to, and we want everything they find sent here first." Another owner leaned back in his chair. "Whatever is in that ground is ours before anyone in that committee wakes up and decides it isn't. You have her ear. Use it." The overseers turned their eyes to the politician and waited.

He did not answer at once. He looked at the screen, at the grey surface that no one yet understood, and his thumb moved along the edge of the folder. "She already thinks the dig is going too fast," he said at last. "She's told me so. Her crew has seen readings off that thing nobody can explain. If I push her in closer and something goes wrong, it's her ship out there, not ours." He hesitated, then went on more quietly. "She has a daughter. I've met the girl."

The room was silent for a moment. One of the overseers tapped a single finger on the black stone. "Your concerns are noted," he said, in a voice that noted nothing. "Now tell her to move in." The politician lowered his eyes to the folder, and on the screen behind the owners the object turned slowly in the red dust.

The politician left the tower with the folder still unopened under his arm, and the next time he spoke of Mars, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="fc67428f-78ef-437b-acb5-3b162b0ed2b2">it was from his seat before the committee, where the members across the table had already begun to speak of halting the excavation.</li>
<li edge="c2bbc7c4-a301-4caa-bcd8-0f0785a8abd3">it was into a crackling link to Varn's corvette in orbit, her face breaking up on the screen as the alien ships closed in behind her.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fa9bb4fc-2c47-4870-824d-182b8bff2a8d" -->

Mars hung below the corvette like a rusted coin, its thin limb glowing where the sun caught the dust. High above the excavation site the two ships of the mission kept their slow orbit together: the lab ship, broad and pale, studded with antennae and sample bays, and a little ahead of it the corvette, narrow and grey, with a single turret on its spine and two light launchers tucked beneath its prow. Through the bridge viewport the lab ship's running lights blinked in patient rhythm, and beyond them the stars lay thick and indifferent.

The message from Earth arrived with the faint chime of a priority channel, and the bridge went quiet. The communications officer read the header twice before he put it on the main screen. Captain Varn stood behind the command chair with one hand on its backrest and read it in silence: *The discovery is to be protected at all costs. Escort vessel will hold position and defend the excavation and the laboratory ship against any approach.* There was no mention of reinforcements and no time given for any. Below the text, the seal of the defense ministry turned slowly in the corner of the screen.

At the tactical station a young lieutenant pulled up the corvette's weapons inventory, as if the numbers might have changed since the last drill. One turret. Eleven missiles. Point defense rated against debris and small craft. He glanced at the captain, then back at his console, and said nothing. Somewhere aft a technician was still running a coolant check, and the soft tick of the cycling pumps carried through the hull.

Varn let go of the chair and stepped up to the viewport. She looked at the lab ship, then out at the black where the sensors had flagged nothing yet, and her jaw tightened. "Acknowledge the order," she said at last, her voice level. "Tell them we'll hold." When the officer had sent it, she stayed where she was, arms folded, watching the empty sky. Everyone on that bridge knew what the corvette was: a guard dog for a research mission, built to chase off scavengers. Its captain knew better than any of them that one weakly armed ship could not stop a fleet.

Long minutes passed on the bridge before the sensor officer _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="3429c43a-2bc8-4d47-9543-4a120028fac9">looked up from her console, her voice tight as she reported that the shapes emerging from the dark past Mars matched nothing in any database the fleet had ever carried.</li>
<li edge="e94e87ee-f5ee-44e2-976c-6812dcb6e900">looked up from her console and shook her head, and Captain Varn, her eyes still fixed on the dark shapes holding station beyond Mars, reached once more for the comms panel.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="4f336a50-5740-468a-ae0a-6eea5bfbb9b9" -->

The committee room sat deep in the old wing of parliament, a long chamber of dark panelling and green leather, its tall windows shuttered against the evening. A wall screen at the far end showed the feed from Mars: the red plain, the scaffolds ringing the excavation, and above them, rendered as cold white points against the black, the ships that had come out of hyper-space and were still coming. Members filled the horseshoe of benches, some leaning toward their neighbours, some staring at the screen. Clerks moved between them with papers no one read. The air smelled of old wood and too many bodies.

A grey-haired member stood first and asked that the excavation be halted at once, the crews brought up, the escort pulled back before anyone fired. Others knocked on their desks in agreement. A younger woman across the floor said the ships had arrived the very hour the digging reached its deepest point, and that no one in the room could call that chance. When the chair called on him, the politician rose slowly, buttoning his jacket, and for a moment he only looked at the screen, at the small marker that stood for Captain Varn's corvette, holding its place between the excavation and the approaching lights.

Then he spoke, and his voice was steady. They did not know what the fleet wanted, he said, but they knew what lay under that soil, and they knew that whoever held it would hold Mars. To abandon it now was to hand it over without a shot. The mission was already there; the escort was already in place. Let it protect the discovery and let the work go on, faster if it could, so that whatever the fleet intended, Earth would have something in its hands when the answer came. He spoke of duty, of the cost already paid, of the shame of retreat. Twice his hand tightened on the edge of the desk, and once he stopped in the middle of a sentence and drew a breath before finishing it. No one on the benches seemed to notice.

The vote was called. The hands went up, slowly at first, then more of them, and the members who had wanted the dig stopped sat back with their arms folded and their faces set. The chair read out the order: the mission would hold its position, protect the discovery, and dig on. A clerk carried it out to be sent. The politician sat down. On the screen the white points drew nearer, and the small marker of the corvette did not move from their path; he watched it for a long time after the room had begun to empty, his hands flat on the leather, saying nothing to the members who passed him on their way out.

Far above the red plain, on the narrow bridge of her corvette, Captain Varn read the order through once and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2c20b181-7179-4df5-b3b5-0bd1ebdf21ea">then opened the private channel to the man who had argued for it, waiting for his face to steady through the delay before she spoke.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="b843f01b-e4f0-4e17-8d9a-4098de7661f0" -->

The school sat at the end of a quiet street lined with plane trees, a low brick building with paper suns taped to its windows and a painted mural of the solar system running the length of the yard wall. Mars had been given a fresh coat of red that term, brighter than the rest. At a quarter past three the gate was crowded with parents and strollers, and the detective stood a little apart from them in a grey coat, a notebook in his pocket he never took out. He had come with a story about a scholarship fund and a few polite questions, and most people were glad enough to talk.

The girl's teacher met him in an empty classroom that smelled of crayons and floor wax. She was a tired woman with chalk on her sleeve, and she spoke of the girl warmly and without hesitation: top of the class in reading, kind to the smaller children, never late. "Her mother's in the Navy, you know," she said. "Gone for months at a time. But when she's here, she's *here*. She came to every parents' evening she could, and when she couldn't, she called in from wherever she was stationed. Once from orbit, with a delay so long we had to take turns talking." She smiled at the memory. "There's no father that I've ever seen. She's done all of it herself."

Outside, by the gate, the neighbours said much the same. An older man walking his dog told him the captain had fixed his fence the week before she shipped out, and that the girl stayed with a sitter across the hall who adored her. A young mother with a toddler on her hip laughed when he asked whether there had been any trouble. "Trouble? That kid says please and thank you to the bus driver. Whatever Varn's doing, she's doing it right." Another woman, buttoning her son's coat, added quietly that she didn't know how the captain managed it, alone, with that job, and that she had never once heard her complain.

The detective thanked them all and walked back down the street under the plane trees. Behind him the children spilled out into the yard, and among them a girl with her school bag slung over one shoulder ran to the sitter waiting by the mural, pointing up at the red planet and saying something he could not hear. He stopped at the corner, watched her a moment longer, and found that his notebook was still empty, and that he had nothing at all to write in it.

That evening, back in his office, the detective laid out the file his client had given him beside the empty notebook, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="4ffc1f56-a07a-4682-8f07-d0ce7e721c86">and began going through the client's own life, page by page, instead of Captain Varn's.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="a1e61b8e-4388-4032-97f0-c5f1354c6074" -->

The courtroom was small and too warm, its tall windows filmed with the grey light of a city afternoon. Rows of benches stood mostly empty. At the front, beneath the seal of the family court, the judge sat with her hands folded over a tablet, and to one side a wide screen hung on the wall, its image grainy and a few seconds behind the room. On it a woman in a naval uniform sat very straight in a cramped metal compartment, a headset pressed to one ear. The clerk at the side table watched the signal-strength numbers in the corner of the screen and made small notes whenever they dipped.

The lawyer rose from the petitioner's table and buttoned his jacket. He did not look at the screen. He walked to the center of the floor and spoke to the bench in a level, almost regretful voice. "Your Honor, no one in this room doubts the respondent's courage. That is not the question before the court. The question is where this child will sleep tonight, and tomorrow, and every night for the length of this assignment." He lifted a printed page and let it hang from his fingers. "The respondent commands an armed escort vessel at Mars. Her own service classifies the posting as hazardous duty. She is not across town, Your Honor. She is not even reachable in real time. She is months away, in a ship built for danger, guarding work no one can promise is safe."

He set the page down and turned at last, briefly, toward the screen, where the woman's image had not yet caught up to his words. "Every day she spends out there, her life is at risk. And every one of those days, her daughter comes home to a house with no parent in it. Not a mother who is late from work, not a mother on a trip, but no parent at all. A child should not have to wait on a light delay to hear whether her mother is alive." He let the silence stretch while, on the screen, the woman's jaw tightened a moment too late, as the words reached her. "We ask only that the court give this girl what her mother, through no fault of character but by her own choice, cannot: someone who is there." The lawyer returned to his table, and the judge bent her head and wrote.

When the judge looked up again, the lawyer was already back on his feet with a thin folder in his hand, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="dfbe00c4-7b1b-4dcb-b8a0-d787076ab381">the photographs inside it, which he had been saving for this moment, held flat beneath his palm as he waited for leave to approach the bench.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5a8dfb57-b2b7-44c1-b459-932ccc07f99e" -->

Mars hung below the corvette like a rusted coin, and above it, where there had been only stars an hour before, the ships kept coming. They did not move as ships should. They slid out of nothing in long ribbons of folded light, settling into shapes that looked grown rather than built: ridged hulls the colour of wet slate, pale seams that brightened and dimmed in a slow rhythm, like breath. On the bridge the sensor crew leaned close to their screens and said nothing useful, because there was nothing useful to say. The readings climbed and fell on every band at once. Whether the glow along those seams was an engine, a weapon gathering charge, or something with no human word at all, no one aboard could tell, and no one on the other escort ships could tell either. The frequencies filled with clipped voices from the other escorts, every one asking the same question.

Captain Varn stood at the comms station with one hand on the back of the officer's chair. "Again," she said. The hail went out on every standard channel, then on the emergency bands, then as a plain carrier pulse repeating a sequence of primes, the sort of thing a child might try. The speakers gave back only static and the soft tick of the clock. "Again." The officer sent it again. The nearest of the alien hulls turned, slowly, until its long axis pointed down toward the dig site on the plain below, and the glow along its flank deepened. Varn watched it without blinking. "Keep sending. Every thirty seconds. Don't stop until I tell you."

The private link from Earth had been open in the corner of her display the whole time, his face frozen in the half-second stutter of the relay. She turned to it at last and spoke evenly, knowing every word would take minutes to cross the dark. "You should hear this from me and not from a report. I've counted forty ships, and more are still coming through. I command one corvette. It has two point-defence batteries and a crew of thirty-one. It cannot stop a fleet. It cannot slow one down. If they mean to come at us, or past us, there is nothing in this orbit that will matter." She paused while the hail went out once more behind her and failed once more. "The order to hold position over the site still stands. I'm following it."

When his answer finally arrived, it came first as silence: his image on the screen, lips parted, saying nothing for a long moment. The colour had gone from his face. Behind him was an office she had never seen, its window full of an ordinary Earth afternoon. He looked down at something out of view, perhaps the very directive with the committee's seal that he had argued for, and then back up at the camera. Varn understood that he was finally seeing what that order had put between her ship and the strangers above Mars. "Varn," he said, and his voice cracked on the delay. "I pushed for that. I'm the one who..." He stopped. On the bridge the comms officer sent the hail again, and the speakers gave back nothing at all.

His words were still crossing the dark toward her when, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1819178c-df4a-4eb6-a648-f3d093f4ae79">in orbit above the excavation, the lab ship's sensor bay had filled with scientists crowding around screens of fresh readings, their mineral scans of the artifact pulled up beside them.</li>
<li edge="a972c2b1-bbae-42d9-bd35-bb2c055f228a">on the corvette's bridge, Captain Varn turned from the screen of incoming ships and opened a channel to the laboratory ship, where the scientists had gone silent over their instruments.</li>
<li edge="08354c6b-b2c5-48b8-a882-c709e76511c4">far below on Earth, in the company's offices, the owners were replaying the recording of the meeting where he had voiced his concerns, and watching his face more closely than his words.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="c808ee00-169f-4a9f-8ca9-32981728e2ef" -->

The office sat above a noodle shop, and the smell of broth and frying oil rose through the floorboards and settled into everything: the files stacked on the radiator, the coat on its hook, the blinds that never quite closed. Rain ran down the one window in crooked lines, and beyond it the city's towers blinked their red warning lights at the low sky. A desk lamp threw a hard yellow circle over the papers spread before the detective, and outside that circle the room went brown and dim.

He had the father's life laid out in pieces. School records, pulled from the district's archive, listed a single parent contact year after year, and it was never the father's name. A visitation schedule from the old divorce, signed and stamped, sat beside a log of the father's travel and bank records that showed him three hundred kilometers away on nearly every weekend the schedule gave him. The detective ran a pen down the dates, ticking each one, and the ticks piled up until he stopped and set the pen down. In four years the father had seen his daughter twice, both times at a birthday, both times for less than an afternoon.

The second folder was thinner. It held transcripts of messages a former drinking friend had been glad to hand over, and the detective read them slowly, his jaw working. *She thinks she can play hero out there and leave me looking like nothing,* one said. *Let's see how she likes it when the kid's gone. This isn't about the girl. It's about making her pay.* Another, sent a week later, was only a row of laughing faces beneath a link to news of the Mars mission.

He sat back, and the old chair creaked under him. For a long while he looked at the photograph pinned to the corkboard across the room: Captain Varn in her dress uniform, standing at a launch gantry with one hand on her daughter's shoulder. Then he pulled the transcripts and the travel log together, squared their edges against the desk, and slid them into a fresh folder, apart from everything his client had paid him to find.

The next morning the detective put on his coat, still smelling of broth, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c685f3e7-908e-4928-a445-631665745967">and took the tram downtown to the courthouse, where the custody hearing was due to begin at nine, and where the envelope of photographs he had handed his client now sat on the lawyer's table.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f5bb1160-52bb-4100-8ca7-7e03e94ec442" -->

The laboratory ship hung in high orbit above Mars, its long spine studded with antennae and sensor blisters, the rust-coloured curve of the planet filling half its viewports. Beyond the planet's limb, where the stars should have been steady, a scatter of dark shapes had appeared out of nothing, and the ship's every instrument was turned toward them. Inside, the main lab had lost all its usual order. Sample trays lay abandoned on the benches, coffee cooled untouched beside keyboards, and the scientists crowded around the displays in twos and threes, talking over one another while the readings scrolled faster than anyone could follow.

At one bank of screens, a geologist had pulled up the mineral scans of the artifact still half-buried in the dust below and set them beside the spectral returns from the alien hulls. The lines matched peak for peak: the same strange lattice, the same isotopic ratios that belonged to nothing in the solar system. A physicist leaned in, traced the overlap with a finger, and swore under his breath. Someone across the room called out that the ships' approach vector pointed straight down at the dig site, and for a moment the room went quiet. Then the arguing began again, louder. The ships had come for the artifact; that much seemed clear. But matching materials proved only a shared origin. Nobody could find a pulse, a transmission, anything to show that the thing in the ground had called them, or that the ships meant to speak at all.

The comm panel chimed, and Captain Varn's face appeared on the main screen, lit by the red glow of her corvette's bridge. Her uniform was crisp, her voice level, but her eyes went from one scientist to the next. "I need to know what they want," she said. "Not what they're made of. What they want."

The lead scientist opened his mouth, closed it, and glanced at the scans still glowing beside him. Behind him the others had fallen silent, some looking at the floor. "We think they're here for the artifact, Captain," he said at last. "The composition matches. The origin is the same. But we don't know whether they're here to take it, to defend it, or for something else entirely. We can't tell you." On the screen, Varn held his gaze a moment longer, then gave a single short nod, and the link went dark.

In the silence the dark screen left behind, the scientists turned back to the scans, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="9e195f4d-061f-41d5-aa19-8a436ea47aca">and the drills on the Martian surface went on biting deeper into the buried object, as if whatever lay beneath the red dust might tell them what the approaching fleet wanted.</li>
<li edge="26909aba-22e1-4369-b492-02c3d9b1a947">while high above them, in orbit, Captain Varn sat alone in her quarters with the corvette's sensor feed still glowing on one side of her desk, and opened the channel to her daughter on Earth.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="28b3afba-0598-42e7-83d8-a53f631a63c1" -->

The company kept its offices on the top floors of a glass tower whose lower levels were rented out to shipping agents and insurance brokers, so that nobody in the lobby had reason to look twice at who rode the private elevator. The boardroom faced west. Across one wall ran a screen showing the Martian dig site in slow, grainy relay: cranes like insect legs, floodlit trenches, and at the bottom of the deepest cut a curve of something dark and smooth that the survey teams had not yet managed to name. Beside it, a smaller display tracked the escort corvette in its orbit, a single green marker labelled with its call sign.

The politician sat at the far end of the long table, his coat still on. Three of the owners faced him, the eldest with a folder closed under one hand, the other two leaning back in leather chairs. A secretary set down a carafe of water and left without being thanked. The eldest owner tapped the green marker on the table's inset glass and said that the corvette was holding too far out. They wanted it lower, close over the trench, close enough that the captain's reports would say the site was secure and the committee would stop asking why the digging needed an armed escort at all.

The politician did not answer at once. When he did, he spoke carefully, and then less carefully. He said that nobody knew what the object was, that the readings from the trench made no sense, that putting a ship and its crew directly above it was a risk no one on the committee would have to take themselves. He said that the captain already had doubts. He said her name twice before he noticed he was doing it, and stopped, and drank some of the water.

The owners exchanged a look that was brief and not hidden. The youngest of them smiled slightly and asked whether it was the crew he was worried about. The eldest opened the folder, glanced at whatever was inside, and closed it again. He thanked the politician for his candor, in the tone of a man noting a figure that did not add up, and said that they would think about it. Nobody stood to show him out. On the wall behind them the green marker held its slow orbit over the red ground, and the politician watched it while the owners began talking among themselves about other things, as if he had already left the room.

He rode the private elevator down alone, and by the time the doors opened onto the lobby _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="c94d3dda-36ed-4d1e-bc58-423e87893859">his phone was already buzzing in his pocket, the committee clerk's number on the screen, and he turned toward the parliament building across the square rather than toward his car.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f2db70df-1db7-4e7c-9c64-78d5aa4c91ae" -->

The family court sat on the fourteenth floor of the old municipal building, in a room panelled in pale wood gone grey at the edges. Rain ran down the tall windows and blurred the city beyond into smears of light. A judge sat high at the front beneath the seal of the district, a clerk at her elbow tapping notes into a console. Along one wall a large screen hung dark and waiting, a small amber light in its corner blinking against the long silence of the link to Mars. The benches were mostly empty, and the few people who filled them kept their coats on against the chill of the air vents.

The detective sat near the back, his hat on his knee, where he could see the whole room without being much seen. He had taken a seat on the aisle out of habit, and he did not lean back. His eyes went from the judge to the father at the front table, who sat with his hands folded and his chin up, and then to the lawyer beside him as she rose and buttoned her jacket.

She carried a slim folder to the bench, drew out a set of prints and laid them one by one before the court while the clerk put each up on the display for the record. The first showed Captain Varn at a café table under a striped awning, half turned toward a well-dressed man who was smiling at her. In the next, the same man held a door for her, his hand light at her back. The lawyer let each one stand a moment before she moved to the next. Then she set down the last of them: the man alone on the steps of a glass-fronted tower, a company name in steel letters above the doors, two broad men in dark suits waiting at the curb. "The petitioner asks the court to consider," she said evenly, "the company the respondent chooses to keep, and the company he keeps." She named the firm, and the inquiries into its ties to organised crime, and the judge wrote something down.

At the back of the room the detective knew every frame. He remembered the cold of the car where he had waited, the click of the shutter, the angle he had chosen so that the faces would be clear. He watched them pass across the screen and turn into something else in the lawyer's voice, and his fingers tightened on the brim of his hat. At the front table, the father did not look at the photographs at all; he looked at the dark screen on the wall, and the corner of his mouth lifted.

The amber light in the corner of the dark screen went on blinking for a long while, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="74a974b1-cdd7-4ec9-aa18-9331e1da67bb">and the detective sat watching it from behind his desk, his own photographs of Varn's daughter spread out before him and the unsigned contract from her ex-husband lying open beside them.</li>
<li edge="fac08b49-6ac7-4efa-aa6e-7fea5e450c82">a link still waiting for Mars to answer, while the courtroom around it filled with murmurs as the clerk carried a fresh bulletin up to the judge's bench.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="034cc98a-8e6b-4c75-b559-49e3b607d51b" -->

The dig site lay in a shallow basin of rust-colored rock, ringed by floodlights that burned against the thin pink daylight. At its center the great trench cut down through layers of ancient sediment, and at the bottom of it, half freed from the stone, lay the object: a long curved surface, darker than the rock around it, faintly ribbed, with a sheen like wet slate though nothing on Mars was wet. Cranes leaned over the edge of the pit. Cables ran down into it from the prefabricated domes that crowded the rim, and along those cables crawled the small orange lights of the drilling rigs.

No one had stopped working. In the main dome the long-range display hung over the consoles, showing the scatter of bright points that had come out of nowhere at the edge of the system and were now drawing closer by the hour. The technicians glanced at it as they passed and then turned back to their screens. Down in the trench, suited figures moved along the scaffolding in pairs, guiding the cutting heads into the stone beside the object's flank. Fine red dust rose and drifted in the low gravity, settling slowly on visors and instruments, and the drills whined on without pause.

The scientists had gathered around a bank of monitors where the sensor arrays fed their readings from the pit. One of them pointed at a trace that rose and fell in a slow, uneven rhythm. "It's stronger since they appeared," she said. "Every time the ships adjust course, this jumps." Another leaned in, frowning, and called over the comm for the crew below to take another core sample from the exposed edge. "If it's talking to them, we need to know what it's saying," he said. "This thing is the only way we'll understand what's coming."

Below, a drill bit into the ribbed surface where it met the rock, and the instruments in the dome flickered all at once. The scientists bent closer to their screens, recording, measuring, calling out numbers to one another, while above them the bright points on the display crept a little nearer, and the work in the trench went on.

Deep in the trench, where the drill had bitten into its flank, the ribbed surface of the object _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="f350041c-4bf5-4924-98dc-681e0e3f11a9">gave a faint shudder, and from somewhere beneath the torn layers a slow, rhythmic pulse began to throb outward into the thin Martian air and the dark beyond it.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fa1ddf58-f79d-41d1-a163-6cda75e80c54" -->

The corvette hung in high orbit above Mars, its hull turned so that the red curve of the planet filled the lower half of every viewport. Inside, the bridge was lit low and amber. The crew kept to their stations, their faces washed pale by the glow of their screens, and the only sounds were the hum of the life-support fans and the clipped murmur of the sensor officers calling out bearings. On the main display, beyond the thin line of the planet's limb, a scatter of bright points had appeared where nothing had been an hour before. They did not move like debris. They held formation.

Captain Varn stood apart from the others, near the communications alcove at the rear of the bridge, one hand braced on the bulkhead. The small screen in front of her showed her daughter's face, slightly grainy and frozen for a moment as the link caught up with itself across the long gap to Earth. Then the girl blinked and leaned closer to her own camera, her hair falling over one eye, a schoolbook still open on the table behind her.

"There are ships," Varn said quietly, keeping her voice level so the crew would not hear the strain in it. "A lot of them. They came out of nowhere, just past the planet. We don't know who they are, and we don't know what they want." She glanced back at the display, at the patient bright points, and then at her daughter again. "I'm telling you because I don't want you to hear it from the news first. And because I'm afraid, sweetheart. I'm afraid they won't stop here. I'm afraid they'll keep going, all the way to Earth."

She waited while her words crossed the dark. Minutes seemed to pass in the seconds of the delay, and behind her a sensor officer called out a new count, higher than the last. On the screen, her daughter's face changed at last, the words arriving all at once, and the girl's mouth opened as though to ask something. Varn did not look away. She pressed her fingers to the edge of the screen, very lightly, as if she could reach through it, and kept her eyes on her child while the ships beyond Mars held their silent line.

Varn drew a slow breath and let her gaze drift back to the bright points beyond the planet's limb, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="8a3009bc-03b8-420c-b153-83549cb831a3">counting them again and measuring the line they drew toward the pale blue speck of Earth, where her daughter slept unaware.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="535dbba7-8767-4df2-9c6e-9411ecc35bfe" -->

The committee room sat deep in the old parliament building, beneath a vaulted ceiling whose painted heavens had dimmed to brown with a century of lamp smoke. Along the curved table the members leaned over their screens, where the latest survey of the Martian dig glowed in pale orange: core samples, depth charts, the dark oval of the object at the heart of it all. An aide moved behind the chairs refilling water glasses, and the clerk at the end of the table murmured the minutes into her recorder in a voice so even it seemed part of the ventilation's hum.

The politician sat three seats from the chair, his jacket buttoned, a pen turning slowly between his fingers. He had been speaking about schedules, about the cost of every week the drills stood idle, when a young staffer slipped through the side door and bent to whisper to the chair. The chair listened, frowned, and held out a hand for the tablet the young man carried. The room quieted the way rooms do when no one has asked them to.

"It seems," the chair said at last, reading, "that this committee has been mentioned in a family court this morning. Or rather, one of its members has." He looked up over his glasses. "A custody hearing concerning the daughter of Captain Varn, who commands our escort at Mars. Counsel for the father raised the captain's association with you." He set the tablet down. "And your association with the company that has applied for the Martian rights."

Heads turned. Someone's chair creaked. A member across the table, who had voted against every acceleration of the dig, folded her arms and did not trouble to hide her small, sour satisfaction. The politician's pen had stopped turning. For a moment he only looked at the tablet, as though he might read it from where he sat, and the light from the survey map lay across his face, orange and still. Then he cleared his throat, said that he would be glad to answer any question the committee wished to put to him, and asked, very politely, whether they might first finish the matter of the schedules.

The chair regarded him for a long moment over the rims of his glasses, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="5b9efebb-935a-4d69-9f0d-1779894a06b6">then lowered his gaze to the folder of evidence on the desk before him, squared its edges, and turned to the members seated along the bench as though to gather their assent before he spoke.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="891661ab-baaf-45e6-8654-b880c0a54f27" -->

The detective's office was on the fourth floor of a building that had been old before the Mars mission was ever announced, and it smelled of cold coffee and printer toner. Rain ran down the single window in crooked lines, blurring the lights of the transit towers beyond. On the desk lay a spread of glossy photographs: Captain Varn in her dress uniform outside a restaurant, Captain Varn laughing beside a man in a tailored coat, the same man holding a car door open for her. Beside them sat a thin folder stamped with the seal of the family court, a copy of the filing in which those same photographs had been entered as evidence.

The ex-husband sat across the desk in the client's chair, one leg crossed over the other, tapping a stylus against his knee. He had come for an update and had been smiling since he walked in. "The judge loved them," he said. "Did you see her face when the lawyer put the one with the car up on the screen? That's the end of it. She can answer from her little ship all she likes." He leaned forward. "Now I want more. Anything else on him, anything on her crew. Whatever you can find."

The detective did not touch the photographs. He opened a drawer, took out the signed contract, and laid it flat on top of them. "I'm done," he said. The ex-husband laughed once, as if at a joke, then stopped when the detective tore the contract cleanly in half and then in half again. "Your retainer's refunded as of this morning. Whatever's left on the account, you'll see it back by the end of the week." The stylus went still. "You can't just walk away from this," the ex-husband said, his voice rising. "I paid you to find dirt on her." "You did," the detective said. "And there wasn't any. There's a woman who calls her kid every night across forty light-minutes, and a man who wants to see her bleed. I know which one I'd leave a child with."

When the door had slammed and the footsteps had faded down the stairwell, the detective sat for a while listening to the rain. Then he pulled the court folder toward him, found the clerk's contact line at the bottom of the filing, and opened a new message. He typed slowly, attaching his notes one by one: dates, times, the recordings of every call in which his former client had asked not about his daughter's welfare but only about what would hurt her mother most. At the top, he addressed it to Captain Varn's counsel, and under the subject line he wrote that he was prepared to testify.

He sent the message, closed the folder over the photographs, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="e55329cf-10bf-4251-bdc3-fa0f41d7ec64">and drove across the city to the politician's office, the file on the seat beside him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="558d9dec-0222-472c-b30e-3b93bf9549c4" -->

The family court sat on the fourth floor of an old civic building, in a room panelled in pale wood that had been varnished so many times it looked wet. Tall windows let in a flat grey light. At the front, beneath the seal, the judge's bench rose above two plain tables: at one sat the father in a pressed suit, his hands folded, and beside him his lawyer with a neat stack of folders and a tablet propped against a water glass. At the other table there was no one but a court officer tending a large screen on a wheeled stand, its corner marked with the slow-blinking icon of the deep-space relay. A few people waited on the benches behind, coats over their knees.

The hearing had stalled on the delay. A question had gone out toward Mars minutes before, and the room was waiting for the answer to crawl back across the distance when a clerk came in from the side door, quickly, and laid a printout in front of the judge. Phones began to buzz along the benches almost at once. Someone in the back row said, too loudly, "Ships. Around Mars," and the officer by the screen bent to his own device, his face changing as he read. The relay icon kept blinking, as though nothing had happened.

The judge read the page twice. Then she set it face down and took off her glasses. "I am told," she said, "that a number of unidentified vessels have appeared in orbit around Mars, and that the mission there is on alert." The father's lawyer did not look surprised so much as attentive; he touched his client's arm, and the father sat straighter, his eyes on the dark screen.

"This court has heard a great deal about duty and about service," the judge went on, choosing each word slowly. "I do not doubt either. But I am not asked to judge a captain. I am asked where a child will wake up tomorrow, and the day after, and next year." She looked at the screen, still waiting on its answer, and then at the father. "A girl needs a parent who will come home. Whatever this news turns out to be, it weighs on that question, and I will not pretend that it does not." The lawyer lowered his eyes to his notes and wrote something brief. On the benches, nobody spoke, and the phones kept humming in their pockets.

The judge folded her hands on the face-down page and, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="546955cd-360f-42f8-b501-205d64cb7154">without looking toward the empty screen where Captain Varn's delayed feed should have been, turned her gaze to the father's side of the courtroom and cleared her throat.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="e63ce80b-8683-49af-b0b2-401bf32001c7" -->

The dig site lay in the bottom of a shallow basin, where the rust-colored plain had been cut open in terraces of raw stone. Floodlights on tall masts threw hard white circles across the pit, and in their glare the object showed through the rock it had slept in: a curved, dark surface, smooth as polished horn, running down past the deepest terrace and out of sight. Drilling rigs stood along its flanks like insects at a wound. Their bits had bored a line of holes into it, and from each hole a thin, glistening seam crept out across the surface and froze in the cold.

The crews worked on in their pressure suits, small figures moving between the rigs and the spoil carts. A team guided a cutting arm down the face of the third terrace, its blade whining against the dark shell, and a fine powder rose from the cut and drifted slowly in the thin air. None of them looked up from their work. The object had never moved, never answered, and the instruments clamped along its length had shown nothing but the faint, steady warmth they had logged since the first day.

Then the warmth changed. Deep inside the shell something tightened, and a slow pulse ran the length of the buried body, so faint that the ground barely trembled under the rigs. Along the cut where the blade still bit, the surface darkened and drew in on itself, the way flesh flinches from a burn. A second pulse followed, stronger, and a third, each one gathering into a single point far below the lowest terrace, until the whole of the thing seemed to be holding its breath.

What left it then was not light and not sound. It passed through the rock and the thin air and the floodlit pit without stirring a grain of dust, a call flung outward past the red sky and the pale sun, beyond the reach of anything the crews had brought to Mars. In it was all the creature had to say: that it was cut, that it was bleeding into the cold, that it could not hold on alone. On the monitors, a column of readings jumped once and fell back to normal, and a technician tapped the screen, frowned, and went back to watching the blade.

Far beyond the pale sun, the call was heard, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="062d2ff2-c158-4457-9e8b-82138eede9e5">and soon the answering ships slipped out of the dark around Mars, their sensors turning toward the human vessels holding orbit above the wounded object.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="4118894e-bf38-4078-8303-d7870e823ad1" -->

The bridge of the corvette hung in the red light of battle stations, and beyond its forward screens Mars turned slowly beneath them, rust and shadow, the dig site a pale scar near the terminator. Above the planet's limb the alien ships had stopped being points of light. They had shape now, long and pale and strangely smooth, and they came on in a loose, unhurried line that the plotting table traced in amber. The crew worked in low voices, calling ranges and closure rates, and one young rating kept wiping his palms on his trousers between keystrokes. Captain Varn stood behind the helm with one hand on the back of the pilot's chair and watched the amber line bend, as she had known it would, toward the inner system and Earth.

She stepped into the small comm alcove off the bridge and keyed the recorder for her daughter. For a moment she only looked at the blinking light. Then she spoke, steadily, the way she spoke to the crew. She said that something was coming and that someone had to be in front of it, and that she was the one who was here. She said she was not sorry to be the one, because it meant there would be more time on the other side, and time was the thing her daughter would need. She told her to brush her hair even on the days she didn't feel like it, to be kind to the man who would come looking in on her, and that she loved her more than every star out the window. Her voice caught once, on the last word, and she let it, and then she sent the message before she could record it again.

The second line had been chiming the whole time. When she opened it, the politician's face filled the screen, gray under bad office lighting, his tie pulled loose. The delay made everything he said arrive a breath too late. He asked her what was happening, and she told him plainly, and he started to talk about the company and the dig and how none of it mattered anymore, and then he stopped. He looked at her for a long moment across the lag, at the red light on her face and the calm in it, and something in his expression came undone. "I love you," he said, as if it surprised him to hear it. "I didn't mean to. I don't know when it started being true."

Varn smiled at him, a small, tired smile, and asked him for only one thing. He said yes before her question had finished reaching him. He promised he would look after her daughter, that he would be there in the mornings and at the school gate and whenever she was frightened, that the girl would not be alone. Behind Varn, the helm called the fleet's range again, closer now, and the pilot turned in his chair to look at her. She nodded to the screen, thanked him, and held his gaze a moment longer while the delay carried his answer toward her.

Then she closed the line, and when she stepped back out onto the bridge, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="557b09dd-bb68-47f2-9735-d514f01c7d81">her crew were already at their stations, every face turned toward the main display, where the alien ships hung between Mars and the distant blue point of Earth.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="f2dad7ad-99aa-4037-bea4-ea4b90b6ed21" -->

The family court sat on the fourth floor of an old municipal building, in a room panelled in pale wood that had been varnished too many times. Rain streaked the tall windows. A clerk tapped at a terminal below the bench, and a court officer stood by the door with his hands folded. On the wall opposite the gallery hung a wide screen. In it, framed by the grey bulkheads of a ship's cabin, sat Captain Varn in uniform, her face still, her eyes fixed a little to one side of the camera. A small amber counter in the corner of the image ticked off the seconds of the delay.

At the left-hand table the father sat upright beside his lawyer, a thin folder squared in front of him. The lawyer had capped her pen and laid it across her notes. Neither looked at the screen. The few people in the gallery had stopped whispering, and the only sounds were the rain and the soft hum of the link.

The judge took off her reading glasses and set them on the papers before her. "I have heard enough to make my position clear," she said. "The child needs a parent who is present and whose circumstances are stable. A posting off-world, in a role of this kind, cannot offer that, and the associations raised in these proceedings give me further concern. I intend to rule for the father." She paused and glanced at the screen. "Captain, you will receive this in a few minutes. I am sorry to deliver it this way."

The father let out a slow breath and smoothed the cover of his folder with one hand. His lawyer gave a small nod, nothing more. On the screen Varn went on sitting as she had, listening to words that had not yet reached her, while the amber counter climbed. The clerk's fingers began to move again on the keys, entering the judge's words into the record.

On the screen, the amber counter went on climbing toward the moment her words would reach Mars, and _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="1259a8c7-0369-4a37-9163-23d3ddcda356">in the hushed courtroom below it the judge laid a hand on the folder that held her ruling, while the politician, alone on the back bench, watched the counter as though it might stop.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="1d6445a8-a8a0-48e0-a69f-e0261f0dbd3b" -->

The committee room sat deep in the old wing of parliament, a long chamber of dark panelling and green leather, its tall windows shuttered against the afternoon. A horseshoe of desks faced a single table at the centre, where a lone microphone stood on a brass stem. Clerks moved along the walls with folders pressed to their chests, and in the gallery above, a handful of reporters leaned forward over the rail, their recorders blinking red.

The politician sat at the centre table with his hands folded on its surface. His suit was pressed and his tie straight, but his eyes were shadowed, and he did not look up at the gallery. Before him lay a single sheet of paper, face down. Around the horseshoe the committee members sat with their own papers squared in front of them, and few of them met his gaze.

The chair cleared her throat and read from the document in her hands, her voice flat and even. The committee had received evidence of undisclosed ties between the member and a mining company under investigation, she said, a company with known links to organised crime. Pending a full inquiry into those ties, the member was suspended from the committee and from all its proceedings, effective immediately. She asked whether he wished to make a statement. The politician leaned toward the microphone, opened his mouth, and then sat back without a word. He shook his head once.

A murmur ran through the gallery, and the scratch of pens rose with it. A clerk stepped forward and laid a printed notice beside the face-down sheet. The politician read it without touching it, then stood, buttoned his jacket, and walked the length of the room toward the doors. No one rose to stop him and no one spoke to him; the committee members bent to their papers as he passed. The doors swung shut behind him, and the chair called the next item on the agenda.

Outside, in the long corridor beneath the portraits of former members, the politician took out his phone and found it already buzzing, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="2be0883d-fcc7-4be9-8a73-526869905e9b">the company's number on the screen and a summons to its offices waiting beneath it, curt and unsigned.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="542b4dda-9164-4e92-9e97-802db0b658f9" -->

Mars hung below the corvette like a rusted coin, its thin air a faint blue rim against the dark. Along the equator, near the great scar of the excavation, the lights of the mining rigs still burned, and the drill shafts still sank toward the thing buried beneath the dust. Above them, in the long slow arc of orbit, the corvette turned, small and grey, its running lights blinking in the patient rhythm of a ship that had been told nothing was wrong.

Beyond it, where the stars had folded open, the fleet waited. The ships were long and pale and smooth as river stones, and they did not move like anything built by human hands; they drifted apart and together as if listening. Thin threads of light passed between them. They studied the scar on the surface, the shafts biting into the living shape below, the rigs that fed them. Then their attention lifted to the ships in orbit: the corvette, the freighters, the tugs and relays strung around the planet. They traced the beams that linked each one to the dig, the orders flowing down and the reports flowing up, and they matched the shapes of the hulls to the machines tearing at the ground. One by one the threads of light settled into a single pattern. These ships were of the same tribe as the ones that wounded what they had come to protect.

On the corvette's bridge, Captain Varn watched the pale ships gather, and saw the slow turn of their prows away from Mars, toward the bright point of Earth beyond the Sun's glare. Her crew had gone quiet at their stations, hands hovering over consoles that could do nothing against such size. Varn stood a moment with one hand flat on the back of the helm chair. Then she gave the order. The engines lit, a hard white bloom behind the hull, and the corvette broke from its orbit and climbed. The crew worked in silence, plotting the burn, feeding power to the drives, as their small ship swung across the stars and came to rest squarely in the fleet's path, its nose turned toward the oncoming wall of pale hulls, a single grey speck standing between them and home.

For a long moment the pale ships hung motionless before the corvette, and then the threads of light between them _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="aef24e87-32b6-45e9-b952-5b5a3c140425">brightened and drew taut, and one by one the alien prows swung away from her toward the crowded ring of ships still holding orbit above the dig site.</li>
<li edge="503fbf1d-dbc3-483a-b1e8-8d59845b525b">began to brighten, gathering slowly towards the single small hull that had placed itself across their path.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="fc867761-f347-462b-a876-704fafe9edb2" -->

The politician's office sat high above the city, all pale wood and tinted glass, and at that hour the light came in low and amber across a desk kept too clean to be worked at. A screen on the far wall ran a muted feed from the Mars mission, red dust and the grey shoulders of machinery. Beneath it, on a side table, a child's drawing in wax crayon had been propped against a stack of committee reports, a lopsided ship with a woman's face in its window.

The detective did not sit when he was offered the chair. He was a heavy man in a coat that had seen rain, and he set a folder on the desk and squared it with two fingers before he let go of it. The politician looked at the folder, then at him, and folded his hands. "You told me on the phone you had something about her," he said. "I'd like to know who's been paying you to have it."

"That matters less than what's in there," the detective said. He opened the folder himself and laid the pages out in a row: school reports, a pediatrician's notes, logged calls from a corvette to a girl's bedroom tablet at the same hour every evening, Earth time, without a single miss. "I was hired to find something wrong with her as a mother. I looked hard. She keeps every appointment she can keep from orbit and arranges the ones she can't. The kid's teachers say she's the parent who answers." He slid a second set of pages forward, thinner, typed messages printed in a cramped font. "And these are his. The father's. Read the ones I've marked."

The politician read. His jaw tightened somewhere around the third page, and he turned it over as if the back might be kinder. The detective watched him the way he watched everyone, and spoke flatly into the quiet. "He doesn't ask how the girl's sleeping. He doesn't ask what she eats. Every time I report, he asks one thing: will it hurt her. Not the daughter. Her." He tapped the marked line, where the father had written that he did not care what it cost so long as the captain lost. "He doesn't want his daughter. He wants a weapon. I thought someone who's been spending his Saturdays with that kid ought to see it." On the wall, the feed from Mars went on silently turning over its red ground, and the politician sat a long while with the pages flat beneath his hands, not looking up at it.

When at last the politician raised his eyes from the pages, the detective had already _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="389e2686-960d-4936-b850-3f643b4fb05b">gathered his photographs and files back into their folder and stood, nodding toward the courthouse doors where the hearing on Varn's daughter was about to resume.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="daf5cd67-8731-45aa-91dd-95529e89cc5c" -->

Above the rust-colored curve of Mars, the orbital lanes had been crowded an hour before: survey tenders, ore haulers hung on their tethers, the long skeletal frame of the supply platform turning slowly against the stars. Now the alien ships hung among them, dark and smooth as river stones, and none of the human vessels moved. The haulers' running lights blinked on in their patient green rhythm, as though nothing had changed.

Then the alien ships fired. There was no flash at the muzzle, only a pale thread of light that touched a tender and the tender came apart, its hull peeling open and venting a white plume that froze into glitter. The threads multiplied. They crossed the lanes in silence, one after another, and the haulers broke along their spines, spilling crates and girders and small tumbling figures in pressure suits who reached for nothing. The supply platform took three hits along its length and folded in the middle, its two halves drifting apart, sparks running along the severed cables. In the space of a few breaths, the busy orbit had become a field of wreckage, glowing and slowly spreading.

Across the fleet's line of approach, Captain Varn's corvette held its position alone. On its bridge the crew worked under the red battle lamps, calling out bearings in clipped voices, and the forward guns spat back at the nearest of the dark ships, bright and small against its bulk. Varn stood at the center of the bridge with one hand on the back of the helmsman's chair, watching the plot, and gave her orders evenly, as if the corvette's weight were enough to turn them aside.

The fleet answered. Several threads of light converged on the corvette at once. Its shields flared violet, held for an instant, and failed; the hull buckled inward around the bridge, and the reactor went in a single soundless bloom that lit the wreckage of the orbit for kilometers around. When the light faded, there was only a widening shell of debris where the corvette had been, and the alien ships moved on through it.

Far from the spreading debris, where no sound of the battle could reach, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="d77fec95-e699-4225-99d7-80a20b962c1a">the automated excavators on the red plain of Mars went on biting into the dust around the artifact, their drills turning in the thin, silent air as they had for weeks.</li>
<li edge="dafc956e-ebbe-4420-827a-0586753e8e49">a message that had crossed the void at the speed of light was still on its way to a quiet house on Earth, where Varn's daughter sat waiting by the screen.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="d6dc8ad9-5e26-4b20-8dfc-b7516d6cdcad" -->

The company kept its offices on the top floors of a glass tower above the river, and the boardroom faced west so that the light came in low and gold across a table long enough to seat thirty. That evening only four chairs were filled. Three of them held the owners, men in quiet suits who sat close together at the far end, a single folder open between them. The fourth held the politician, alone at the near end, his coat still on, his hands folded on the polished wood as if he expected to be leaving soon.

Through the glass wall behind him, staff moved along the corridor with tablets and coffee, glancing in and then away. Nobody knocked. On a screen mounted at the end of the room, a map of the Martian dig glowed in muted orange, the excavation marked in red lines and figures that no one in the room looked at.

The eldest owner turned a page in the folder without reading it. "You have done good work for us," he said, in the even voice of a man reading terms. "The committee listened to you. The digging went faster because of you. But something has changed." He slid a photograph across the table: the politician and a girl on a park bench, the girl laughing at something he had said. "You call her ship every night. You argue for her caution in rooms where you were meant to argue for ours. You have grown too attached to Captain Varn."

The politician looked at the photograph for a long moment and did not touch it. "She has concerns about the dig," he said. "They are not unreasonable." The second owner smiled thinly at that, as though the words proved the point. "That is exactly the problem," he said. "A man who repeats her concerns is no longer our man. He is a liability." The third owner closed the folder. "Your arrangement with us ends tonight. Your access ends tonight. Security will see you down." The politician rose slowly, buttoned his coat, and stood a moment longer at the table, his eyes on the girl in the photograph. Then he left it where it lay and walked out past the glass, where two guards had already fallen in behind him.

Behind him, in the emptied boardroom, the map of the Martian dig still glowed on the screen at the end of the table, and the three owners had only begun to talk among themselves when _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="afe0500e-e187-43ea-a53a-edbc76998d46">the feeds from the excavators along the bottom of the map began to stutter, one telemetry line after another freezing on its last reading while the owners leaned toward the screen and the room went quiet.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="0f53b526-ba10-46de-ad01-abd9b13a060b" -->

The dig site lay in a shallow basin at the foot of a broken ridge, where the rust-colored ground had been peeled back in terraces, each one cut cleaner and deeper than the last. Floodlights on tall masts burned against the thin pink daylight, throwing long, doubled shadows across the pit. At its bottom, half freed from the rock, something dark and smooth curved out of the stone, too regular to be a boulder and too strange to be anything else. Around it the automated excavators worked in a slow ring: squat, six-wheeled machines with jointed arms, their cutting heads grinding into the bedrock and sending up fine plumes of dust that drifted and settled in the weak gravity.

Then, one after another, they stopped. The nearest rig froze with its arm lifted, the drill head still spinning down to silence. The next one along halted mid-turn, its wheels locked in the regolith. Within a few heartbeats the whole ring stood motionless, and the only movement left in the pit was the dust, sinking slowly back onto the machines and onto the dark curve of the thing they had been cutting toward.

In the pressurized control module at the rim, the technicians on shift leaned toward their screens. Status lights that should have shown green blinked amber, then held there. One of them tapped at a console and read the same line aloud twice: no fault reported, no command received, every unit simply idle. Another pulled up the external cameras and cycled through them, rig by rig, each feed showing the same still arm, the same settling haze. "Send the restart," someone said, and the restart was sent, and nothing in the pit so much as twitched.

Out on the terraces, a pair of suited workers who had been checking a power line straightened and turned toward the silent machines. They stood with their helmets angled down into the excavation, their radios crackling with questions from the module that neither of them could answer. Below them the rigs waited in their ring, arms raised, and the floodlights hummed on over the half-buried shape, lighting it as brightly as ever in the sudden quiet.

Many millions of kilometers away, in the dim command center on Earth where the long-range tracking boards glowed across the far wall, the officers on watch _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="848d2734-d949-4487-8221-e0f393a4f777">leaned closer to the boards as one by one the bright markers of the Mars fleet flickered, and the senior officer reached for the line to command.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="60dfbb85-fb12-4816-b26e-108ee4d300b2" -->

The company's offices took up the top three floors of a tower of black glass, and on the highest of them the operations room never darkened. A long wall of screens carried the feeds from Mars: the red pit of the dig seen from a mast camera, columns of telemetry from the drill rigs, the slow green pulse of each excavator's beacon on a map of the crater. Below the screens sat a row of technicians in grey company shirts, headsets on, coffee going cold beside their keyboards. Behind them, in a glassed-off room with a view of the city, a handful of men in good suits had stopped pretending to work and stood watching the wall.

The first beacon went out without a sound. A technician leaned toward her screen, tapped it, and called the rig by its number twice before she raised her hand. Then a second dot winked and was gone, and a third, and the mast camera's picture tore into bands of colour and froze on a half-frame of dust. Voices rose along the row. One operator pulled up the relay satellite and read off its status in a flat voice: still transmitting, still receiving, nothing coming back from the surface. Another kept sending the same handshake into silence, over and over, his lips moving with it.

"Get them back," one of the suited men said, coming out of the glass room. "Every channel. Now." The technicians tried. They routed through the backup relay, then through the escort's frequencies, then through anything the company had ever leased. The map of the crater emptied dot by dot until only the outline of the pit remained, a ring of pale lines on a dark screen. Somebody read out the minutes since the last packet, and then stopped reading them out.

In the glass room a phone was ringing, and no one went to answer it. One of the men had taken a thick folder from the table, the licence drafts with their tabs and signatures, the exclusive rights to Mars set out clause by clause, and he held it as though he did not know where to put it down. Another stood at the window with his back to the screens. "Without a working site there's nothing to license," he said quietly, to no one. "They'll give it to whoever's left standing." On the wall, the frozen half-frame of red dust held a moment longer, and then the feed went black.

Across the city, in a courthouse corridor where the news had not yet arrived, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="a7a17ba3-6402-4138-9f24-61c23e789fc0">the politician sat on a hard bench outside the custody hearing, turning his phone over in his hands and waiting for word from Mars.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="37db7f99-45e1-4c18-a41f-b88784d77528" -->

The command center lay three levels beneath the ground, a long room lit mostly by its own screens. Rows of consoles faced a wall-sized display where the inner solar system turned in slow, pale lines: the blue dot of Earth, the rust dot of Mars, and between them the thin traces of every registered vessel, each tagged with a name and a heading. The air smelled of recycled heat and cold coffee. Officers in grey uniforms sat hunched at their stations, headsets pushed back on one ear, murmuring numbers to one another in the flat voices of people at the end of a long watch.

The first alarm was small, a single tone from the tracking section. A young technician leaned toward her screen, frowned, and tapped at it as if the fault might be in the glass. The tag she had been following near Mars had gone dark. Before she could call it out, a second tag went, then a third, and the tone repeated itself until it became one unbroken sound. On the great display, the bright cluster of traces around Mars thinned and vanished, one after another, leaving only the planet and a scattering of strange returns no one could name.

"Telemetry's gone," someone said. "All of it. Not drifting, gone."

"Check the relays," the watch commander ordered, already on his feet. He was a heavy man with a close-cut beard, and he stood very still in the center of the room while reports came back from every side: the relays were sound, the signal paths were clear, the last packets from the escort and the excavation craft had ended in mid-sentence. One officer read aloud the final fragment, a burst of static and the beginning of a word that was never finished. Then the room went quiet except for the machines.

The commander looked up at the empty space around Mars for a long moment. "Those ships didn't fail," he said at last. "Nothing fails all at once like that. Someone took them." He turned to the communications desk. "Get me the Joint Chiefs and the Council. Tell them we've been attacked." Around him, the officers bent back over their consoles, faster now, their faces lit blue and white, and someone began to read out the time stamp so that it could be written down as the moment it had begun.

<!-- cell: markdown id="73e39be9-3ae6-410f-9ce5-840416b6b1a4" -->

The house stood quiet at the end of its street, the porch light still burning though the afternoon had not yet failed. Inside, the kitchen held the small disorder of a household run by one person: a cereal bowl left in the sink, a jacket over a chair back, a calendar on the refrigerator with a red circle drawn around a date weeks away and the word *home* written beneath it in a careful adult hand. Model ships hung on fishing line from the ceiling of the hallway, turning slowly in the draft from a window left open a crack.

Varn's daughter sat cross-legged on the living-room floor with the console balanced on her knees, homework spread around her on the carpet. When the chime sounded she grabbed for it with both hands, already smiling, because only one person ever sent messages on that channel. The screen took a long moment to resolve. Then her mother's face filled it, framed by the gray metal of a ship's bridge, the light on her skin red and flickering, voices in the background calling out numbers that meant nothing to the girl.

Captain Varn did not smile at first. She looked straight into the camera as though she could see through it, and when she spoke her voice was low and even, the voice she used for bad news and bedtime. She said she did not have long. She said there were ships coming, a great many of them, and that someone had to stand between them and home, and that her ship was the one that was there. She said she was sorry she would miss the date on the calendar. Then she did smile, crookedly, and told her daughter that she was brave and clever and loved more than anything in the whole of space, that none of this was her fault, and that she should never once think it was. Behind her, someone shouted, and the picture shook. Varn glanced away, then back, and pressed two fingers to her lips and touched them to the lens. "Be good," she said. "I love you. Goodbye, sweetheart."

The screen went dark, and the channel light blinked twice and went out. The girl sat without moving, the console still held up in front of her face, waiting for the picture to come back. In the hallway the model ships went on turning on their strings. After a while she lowered the console into her lap and pressed the replay key, and her mother's face appeared again in the red light of the bridge, saying she did not have long.

<!-- cell: markdown id="1b0a2dcc-a462-4623-9c50-72f8e7718010" -->

The courtroom had been built for quieter days: pale wood benches, a high bench of darker wood, tall windows that let in a flat grey light. On the wall behind the judge, a screen that had carried a delayed link to a ship near Mars for the last hearings now showed only the court's seal. The father sat at one table with his lawyer, papers squared in front of them. At the other, the politician sat alone, his tie loosened, his hands pressed flat against the table as if to keep it from moving. The detective sat near the back, a slim case on his knees, and watched the clerk move along the rows with a tablet held against her chest.

The murmur began before anyone spoke aloud. Phones lit up along the benches; a bailiff bent to read something over a reporter's shoulder and straightened slowly. The detective turned his own screen face up. Every ship Earth had sent to Mars was gone, it said, the escort and the survey vessels with it, and the government was calling it an act of war. He read the list of ships twice. Varn's corvette was on it. Across the room the father's lawyer had read the same thing and leaned in to whisper; the father's mouth moved, and something in his face eased that should not have eased.

The judge called the room to order, and the clerk confirmed for the record what the screens already said: Captain Varn's ship was lost, with no word of survivors. The politician stood before he was asked to. His voice was rough but steady. "She asked me to look after her daughter," he said. "If anything happened. I'm asking the court to let me do what she asked." The father's lawyer was already rising to object, citing the man's ties, his backers, the photographs of him and the captain that had been entered weeks before.

The detective stood up in the back row. "Your Honor, I took those photographs," he said, and the room turned to look at him. He walked to the front, opened the case, and laid out what he had: the contract the father had signed with him, the messages asking for anything that would hurt her, the notes he had kept after he stopped looking at Varn and started looking at the man who paid him, the record of afternoons the politician had spent with the girl while her mother was at Mars. "I was hired to find dirt on her. There wasn't any. Everything I found is about him." The father's lawyer said nothing. The judge took the papers, read in silence for a long while, and the politician stood with his head bowed, waiting.

The judge set the last page down on the bench, _____

<ol title="choices">
<li edge="93f1d7a7-bd76-466a-9d1b-c096ed1f94e3">and looked across the courtroom at the detective's file still open beside it, then at the father, and last at the lawyer who had argued so hard for him.</li>
</ol>

<!-- cell: markdown id="5664354c-85b4-4adb-be36-13f7ad14f844" -->

The courtroom was smaller than the cases it had heard: four rows of benches, a raised bench of pale wood, and a wall screen that had once carried a voice from very far away and now showed only the seal of the court. Rain ran down the tall windows. At one table sat the father in a dark suit, his lawyer beside him with her files squared to the edge. At the other sat the politician, his tie loosened, and next to him a girl of about ten who held a folded paper crane in both hands and did not look up.

The detective stood at the witness stand with a tablet in his hand. He did not hurry. He set out messages, dated and timed, and read them aloud in a level voice: the father asking whether the photographs were bad enough to make her cry, the father asking whether they could be released the week she was due back, the father writing that he did not want the girl, only for her mother to lose her. The judge asked for the screen, and the words went up one after another in plain white text. Someone in the back row let out a breath. The father's lawyer half rose to object, then sat down again and did not finish the word.

The judge read for a long time. When she spoke, it was briefly. The court found that the petition had been brought to injure the girl's mother and not for the good of the child; it was dismissed. Custody was granted to the man the mother had named in her own wishes, who had cared for the girl these past months and asked for nothing else. The politician bowed his head. The girl looked at him at last, and he put his hand flat on the table near hers, not quite touching, until she slid the paper crane across to him.

The father stood and left without a word to anyone, the door swinging behind him. His lawyer stayed in her seat. She gathered her files slowly, one by one, and stopped at the last of them, the photographs she had laid before this same bench, the arguments she had made about danger and bad company and a mother unfit to raise her child. She looked across at the girl and the man bent toward her, then down at the screen where the messages still glowed, and she understood with a cold clarity whose cause she had carried into the room, and what it had wanted. She closed the folder and sat with her hands on it while the court emptied around her.
